=== Page 2 === Enhancing Programming Education with Open -\nSource Generative AI Chatbots  \nAntonia Šarčević  *, Ivan Tomičić  *, Andrija Me rlin *, Marko Horvat  * \n* University of Zagreb, Faculty of Electrical Engineering and Computing, Department of Applied Computing, Zagreb, \nCroatia  \nantonia.sarcevic@fer.hr , ivan.tomicic@fer.hr , andrija.merlin@fer.hr, marko.horvat3@fer.hr   \n \n \nAbstract  — This paper describes the development of an \nOpen -Source Generative AI Chatbot, utilizing free Large \nLanguage Models (LLM) to enrich the student learning \nexperience for a university course in “Introduction to \nProgramming ”. The article aims to provide a step -by-step \nguide for selecting, fine -tuning, and evaluating available \nmodels.  As a first step in choosing the appropriate LLM, \nwhich provides the mos
y-step \nguide for selecting, fine -tuning, and evaluating available \nmodels.  As a first step in choosing the appropriate LLM, \nwhich provides the mos t accurate responses while not \nrequiring excessive computing power, the article will cover a \ndiscussion of the  advantages and disadvantages of local vs. \ncloud -available models. After selecting a few promising \nmodels, the next stage includes fine -tuning LLMs to answer \ndomain -specific questions using a dataset containing essential \nrules, guidelines, and explanatory content regarding the \nsubject. The crucial aspect of selecting a model was evaluating \nanswers, and in this context, both human and automatic \nevaluation techniques will be presented.  Finally, it is possible \nto enhance the model performance and accuracy by \nincorporating Retrieval -Augmented Generation (RAG) \ntechniques and exploring the influence of various factors, such \nas different vector databases, model temperat
Retrieval -Augmented Generation (RAG) \ntechniques and exploring the influence of various factors, such \nas different vector databases, model temperat ures, maximum \ntoken lengths, prompt templates, embeddings, repetition \npenalties, and chunking siz es. Our results show that chatbots \nhave significant potential to improve academic support and \nlearning efficiency, as well as personalized education in \ngeneral.  \nKeywords – chatbots; generative models; large language \nmodels; natural language processing; education; digital \nlearning  \nI.  INTRODUCTION  \nThe transition to university life always  represents a \ncritical stage in a higher  education, especially for first -year \nstudents  dealing with  demanding coursework in , what is to \nthem,  new and unfamiliar  university  environments . \nFreshmen computer science (CS) and computer engineering \n(CE) students often need help and clarification even with the \nbasic courseware issues, such a
reshmen computer science (CS) and computer engineering \n(CE) students often need help and clarification even with the \nbasic courseware issues, such a s the number of points \nrequired to achieve a specific grade, or how to install a \nplugin in the required programming working environment.  \nAlthough information they require is made available to the \nstudents through the official course material and web pages, \nmany of them prefer direct communication with academic \nstaff. On the other hand, teachers and other faculty members \nare outnumbered by the growing number of students, and the \ngap seems to be widening as teachers face staff shortages.  \nThis ubiquitous problem has sparked the development of \nmany novel digital solutions for support of academic \nteaching, such as the automated programming assess ment \nsystems [1][2]. Recognizing th ese issues , we developed a user -friendly \nchatbot that uses open-source  artificial intelligence (
mming assess ment \nsystems [1][2]. Recognizing th ese issues , we developed a user -friendly \nchatbot that uses open-source  artificial intelligence ( AI)  \nLarge Language Model (LLM) [3] to assist freshmen \nstudents with their academic questions  on an obligatory  first-\nsemester course “Introduction to programming” [4] taught at \nUniversity of Zagreb, Faculty of Electrical Engineering and \nComputing . The course teaches fundamental concepts such \nas data and control structures, functions, arrays, and file \nhandling. It also covers basic software engineering \nprinciples such as algorithm design, decomposition, \ndocumentation, and the software development li fecycle, \ngiving students the skills  to succeed in practical \nprogramming and problem -solving.  \nThe goal of this paper is to thoroughly describe the \nprocess of  theoretical exploration and hands -on training of \nLarge Language Models (LLMs) as part of our project.  Our \nmotivatio
ghly describe the \nprocess of  theoretical exploration and hands -on training of \nLarge Language Models (LLMs) as part of our project.  Our \nmotivatio n is to share  the results and methodologies  of the \nproject , thereby facilitating the development of similar AI -\nbased solutions for educational purposes  by other \nresearchers . The project was carried out by a joint team of \n16 undergraduate and graduate students from the University \nof Zagreb, Faculty of Electrical Engineering and Comp uting  \nunder supervision of their selected mentor  as the fourth \nauthor . The team leaders of undergraduate and graduate \nstudent teams were the first and second author, respectively.  \nTo be able to successfully complete the project we \nconsidered all open LLMs available in October 2023. For \ninjecting domain -specific knowledge into LLM, we \nexplored both fine -tuning and knowledge augmentation \nmechanisms like Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)
g domain -specific knowledge into LLM, we \nexplored both fine -tuning and knowledge augmentation \nmechanisms like Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)   \n[5][6]. Our findings favor RAG over fine -tuning for its \nsuperior performance in handling existing and new \nknowledge across different problem  domains [7][8]. \nThe remainder of the paper is organized as follows; \nSection 2 examines LLM selection criteria, focusing on \narchitecture, courseware training data, and performance. \nSection 3 evaluates local versus cloud LLMs against \ntechnical and financial considerations. Section 4 outlines our \nmethodology for training and fine -tuning LLMs with \nspecific datasets. Section 5 assesses model effectiveness \nusing human and automated metrics. Section 6 discusses \nRAG and its impact on chatbot performance. Finally, we \nshare our find ings and recommendations for LLM chatbot \ndeployment in education.  \nII. MODEL SELECTION  \nThe initial step in o
formance. Finally, we \nshare our find ings and recommendations for LLM chatbot \ndeployment in education.  \nII. MODEL SELECTION  \nThe initial step in o ur project involved an in -depth \nexploration of available LLMs  – both commercial and \nMIPRO 2024/SP\n2367
