=== Page 3 === located in the cloud , and free and accessible locally – \nfocusing on understanding the current landscape of high -end \nlanguage models. The factors we considered  are: \navailability, price per token, model architecture, training \ndata requirements , reported past performance, and fine -\ntuning capabilities. This comprehensive understanding was \ncrucial in identifying key characteristics that would \ninfluence the effectiveness  and usability  of LLMs . After an \nexhaustive  valuation process, we carefully selected a group \nof model s that demonstrated not only the ability to \ncomprehend but also produce d high-quality, contextually \nrelevant responses  to courseware questions . \nA. Local vs cloud -available LLMs  \nOur exploration extended to considering models of \nvarious sizes, both cloud
responses  to courseware questions . \nA. Local vs cloud -available LLMs  \nOur exploration extended to considering models of \nvarious sizes, both cloud  -available and locally run  [3][9]. \nMultiple cloud -based LLMs were tested, using cloud \nplatforms such as  the AI-powered  open platform  Poe.com  \n[10], alongside locally run LLMs from Hugging Face  online \nrepository [11]. This adapted  approach allowed us to \nthoroughly assess the performance of different models in \ninjecting domain -specific knowledge. However, considering \nour infrastructure limitations, very large models could not be \nchosen because of the computing power necessary to use \nthem, and cloud -based models were unfit as their usage is \noften too expensive. In line with that, we selected a group of \n10 perspective locally run LLMs of roughly the same size (7 \nbillion parameters)  that should be further evaluated to select \none optimal model . \nB. Training and testing
run LLMs of roughly the same size (7 \nbillion parameters)  that should be further evaluated to select \none optimal model . \nB. Training and testing   \nFollowing the initial model selection, the hands -on \ntraining phase commenced, where our team engaged in a \nprocess of training on the official documentation of the \n“Introduction to Programming ” course, a n obligatory \nfreshman  course  at the undergraduate Study Programmes  \nComputing and Electrical Engineering and Information \nTechnology  [4]. Specifically, pre-trained LLMs were fine-\ntuned  utilizing  available course  materials contain ing \ninstructions for installing local extensions, instructions for \ncommand prompt environment,  instructions for performing \nlaboratory exercises, and the overall student guide . In total, \nthe LLMs were fine-tuned  on a dataset cont aining  6 \ndocuments with 12.828 words.  \nThe objective was to equip each of the 10 selected  \nlanguage model s with
ere fine-tuned  on a dataset cont aining  6 \ndocuments with 12.828 words.  \nThe objective was to equip each of the 10 selected  \nlanguage model s with  a foundational understanding of the \nsubject matter, enabling them to generate accurate and \ncontextually relevant responses to user queries. The models \nwere then tested on a set of 20 questions used to narrow the pool of models. The answers given by the models were \nevaluated by our student team. It was immediately clear that \nsome models performed significantly worse than others. \nThis brief evaluation was not precisely structured, but a \nrough review of models that gave us a clear picture of which \nmodels should be further tested and which should be \ndiscarded. Therefore,  we continued our evaluation with  a \nsubgroup consisting of  the following 5 models:  \n1. llama -2-7b-chat.Q5_K_M  \n2. mistral -7b-instruct -v0.1.Q5_K_M  \n3. mistral -7b-openorca.Q4_0  \n4. zephyr -7b-alpha.Q5_K_M
following 5 models:  \n1. llama -2-7b-chat.Q5_K_M  \n2. mistral -7b-instruct -v0.1.Q5_K_M  \n3. mistral -7b-openorca.Q4_0  \n4. zephyr -7b-alpha.Q5_K_M  \n 5. zephyr -7b-beta.Q5_K_M  \nTo further scrutinize the shortlisted models, we \nexpanded the set of  evaluation  questions, introducing a \nspectrum of difficulty levels. We deliberately included \nquestions with contexts outside the model's training data and \nthose containing grammatical errors to assess the robustness \nof the models. In the end, the full list contained N = 92 \nquestions related to the “Introduction to Programming ” \ncourse. The questions varied in difficulty, ranging from \nsimple, straightforward queries to  more complex ones \nrequiring logical reasoning  and arithmetic calculations . This \ndiverse set of questions was then fed to the trained LLMs, \nand their responses were meticulously recorded and \nevaluated by us based on predefined metrics.  In this step, t he \nwhole gro
to the trained LLMs, \nand their responses were meticulously recorded and \nevaluated by us based on predefined metrics.  In this step, t he \nwhole gro up of 16 students in the team participated in the \nevaluation , posing all N = 92 questions to the selected 5 \nmodels . This step is described in more detail in the next \nsubsection.  It is important to note that all  model names \nfollow  the GGML naming convention, with parameter b \nindicating the model's size in billions of parameters and Q, \nK, and M corresponding to different weight quantization \nlevels. Quantization is a technique that enables LLMs to run \non consumer hardware  utilizing only  CPU inference.  \nC. Evaluation  \nThe evaluation phase involved grading the models based \non five critical categories: fluency, coherence, relevance, \ncontext understanding, and overall quality. Each model \nreceived a Likert -scale grade from 1 to 5 in each category, \nproviding a nuanced assessme
e, \ncontext understanding, and overall quality. Each model \nreceived a Likert -scale grade from 1 to 5 in each category, \nproviding a nuanced assessme nt of its performance. To \nestablish a benchmark for evaluation, we created a list of 3 -\n5 expected answers for each question in the extended set. \nThese expected answers were carefully curated b y our team, \nleveraging the context used for training the LLMs.  The \nresults of the evaluation  are shown in Table I.   \n \nTABLE I.  AVERAG E GRADE PER CATEGORY RESULTS FOR EACH OF THE 5 EVALUATED  MODELS\nModel  Fluency  Coherence  Relevance  Context \nunderstanding  Overall \nquality  Average \ntotal  \nllama -2-7b-chat.Q5_K_M  4.87 4.79 4.15 4.09 3.95 4.35 \nmistral -7b-instruct -v0.1.Q5_K_M  4.80 4.68 4.34 3.73 3.70 4.25 \nmistral -7b-openorca.Q4_0  4.92 4.88 4.38 4.44 4.48 4.62 \nzephyr -7b-alpha.Q5_K_M  4.81 4.71 4.43 3.84 3.67 4.29 \nzephyr -7b-beta.Q5_K_M  4.87 4.75 4.18 4.15 4.15 4.42 \n2368\nMIPR
