=== Page 4 === Figure 1.  Box-plot with Answering Time per each of the 5 evaluated model s\nIn this phase of our work, we emphasized the importance \nof depth in the evaluation of these models. Apart from \nhuman evaluation, we calculated BLEU [12], ROUGE [13] \n[14], and diversity scores to provide more objective insight \ninto the effectiveness of the model. The metrics compare the \nanswer generated by the LLM with the corresponding \ncorrect answer to the question (our list of 3 –5 expected \nanswers). It is good practice to perform these metrics to gain \nan objective statistical basis for making decisions in a \nproject. In our case, the metrics did not result in high scores, \nbut that can be common when the model’s answer does not \nmatch the expected one word -for-word.  \nConsidering all evaluation criteria
in high scores, \nbut that can be common when the model’s answer does not \nmatch the expected one word -for-word.  \nConsidering all evaluation criteria , our choice of the best \nmodel was based on the human evaluation, and by that \nMistral -7B-Openorca significantly outperformed other \nmodels . \nIII. RAG  CONFIGURATIONS  \nThe centerpiece of our project was using Retrieval \nAugmented Generation (RAG) to enhance the performance \nof natural language processing (NLP) tasks, such as \nanswering questions  [5]. By using RAG, our chatbot gains \naccess to more useful information from a database larger \nthan the text it was trained on  [5]. Essentially, RAG is a \nknowledge augmentation mechanism for LLMs, it \nsupplements their inherent capabilities with information \nretrieved from external knowledge sources  [5].  \nTo improve this method and find the optimal \nconfiguration we concluded detailed research on this \ntechnique and implemented and tested
ources  [5].  \nTo improve this method and find the optimal \nconfiguration we concluded detailed research on this \ntechnique and implemented and tested  various combinations \nof different retriever types, chunking sizes and overlaps, \nnumber of retrievals, and different embedd ings. To \nunderstand how this works, a brief explanation of RAG is \nneeded.  \nA. Retrieval -Augmented Generation  \nRetrieval -Augmented Generation (RAG) is a \nmethodology used to enhance the capabilities of Large \nLanguage Models (LLMs) by incorporating knowledge from external databases  [15]. It addresses challenges faced \nby LLMs, such as hallucination, outdated knowledge, and \nnon-transparent reasoning processes, by seamlessly \nintegrating domain -specific information. RAG improves the \naccuracy and credibility of models, particularly in \nknowled ge-intensive tasks, by synergistically merging the \nintrinsic knowledge of LLMs with the vast, dynamic \nrepositories o
odels, particularly in \nknowled ge-intensive tasks, by synergistically merging the \nintrinsic knowledge of LLMs with the vast, dynamic \nrepositories o f external databases. This comprehensive \napproach allows for continuous knowledge updates and \nfacilitates the integration of domain -specific  information  [6]. \nRAG is the process of optimizing the output of a large \nlanguage model, so it references an authoritative knowledge \nbase outside of its training data sources before generating a \nresponse [5]. RAG implements three main components: 1) \ndocument chunking, 2) embedding, and 3) searching [16]. \nChunking involves dividing documents into parts of \narbitrary size [16]. The goal of this process is to achieve a \nbalance between the amount of information and the amount \nof noise contained in each chunk. Intuitively, choosing a \nchunk that is too large results in an excessive amount of \ninformation, and therefore noise, that c annot be re
n each chunk. Intuitively, choosing a \nchunk that is too large results in an excessive amount of \ninformation, and therefore noise, that c annot be re presented \nas a vector of a specific size. On the other hand, choosing a \nchunk that is too small leads to dispersed information across \nmany chunks, which may be difficult to manage. In this \ncontext, we implemented small chunk sizes of 500 tokens \nwith an overlap of 50 tokens, medium of 1000 tokens with \nan overlap 200, and large chunks of 2000 tokens with an \noverlap of 400.  \nEmbedding involves representing the obtained chunks as \nvectors of a specific size [16]. To convert text into vectors, \nwe use a method called contextual embedding. We wish to \nretain the word order – sentences can take on different \nmeanings depending on the word order. We also wish to \nretain the context, because the same word can have a \ndiffe rent representation depending on the words it is \nsurrounded by, mea
der. We also wish to \nretain the context, because the same word can have a \ndiffe rent representation depending on the words it is \nsurrounded by, mea ning that sentences containing similar \nwords can have significantly different vectors. In our \nexperimentation, we employed the three following \nembeddings: 1) all -MiniLM -L6-v2 which maps sen tences \nand paragraphs to a 384 -dimensional dense vector space and \nMIPRO 2024/SP\n2369
ee following \nembeddings: 1) all -MiniLM -L6-v2 which maps sen tences \nand paragraphs to a 384 -dimensional dense vector space and \nMIPRO 2024/SP\n2369 === Page 5 ===
=== Page 5 === offers both speed and quality, 2) Jina embedding which \nsupports longer sequence lengths, and 3) BAAI general embedding that can map any text to a low -dimensional dense \nvector.  \n \n \nFigure 1.  An overview of a typical RAG arhitecture\nIn the searching  component  vector representations of \nqueries and document chunks are used to find relevant \ndocuments. Metrics like L2 -Norm and cosine similarity \nmeasure the distance or similarity between vectors, focusing \non properties like length or direction  [17][18]. Indices are \nthen employed to search the vector database for relevant \ndocuments. Different methods, such as exhaustive search \nand Voronoi cells, balance search speed, accuracy, and \nmemory usage. Exhaustive search checks all vectors, \nguaranteeing  perfect search quality but scalin
ronoi cells, balance search speed, accuracy, and \nmemory usage. Exhaustive search checks all vectors, \nguaranteeing  perfect search quality but scalin g with vector \nnumber and dimension. Voronoi cells group neighboring \nvectors to expedite search, with fewer cells to explore \ncompared to vectors. This approach reduces search \ncomplexity but relies on efficient indexing. In our work we \nused L2 distance metric.  \nTo test this method more comprehensively, we evaluated \nvarious types of retrievers, including parent retrievers that \nretrieve a broader set of documents and rerank retrievers that \nrefine the results based on relevance. Within this framework, \nwe explored di fferent combinations of the number of \nretrievals and the top number of retrievals, such as 5 \nretrievals with top 3 documents, 7 retrievals with top 2, and \n10 retrievals with top 4 documents.  \nB. Evaluation  \nIn the end, we had 27 configurations with rerank \nretriever a
nts, 7 retrievals with top 2, and \n10 retrievals with top 4 documents.  \nB. Evaluation  \nIn the end, we had 27 configurations with rerank \nretriever a nd 2 with parent retriever. Each configuration was \ngiven 30 questions, a shortened list of the original  group of  \n92. The answers to each configuration were evaluated by our team based on new criteria focusing on expression and logic, \naccuracy and relevance, and overall quality and engagement. \nThis approach not only ensures a thorough analysis of the \nmodel's linguistic capabilities but also delves into its \ncontextual understan ding and engagement wit h users.  \nThe expression and logic evaluation criteria provide an \nassessment of the model's ability to formulate answers \nclearly and coherently. This analysis, which is divided into \nfive levels from low to high, provides an indication of the \nmodel's ability to conve y information effectively.  \nAccuracy and relevance as further eva
ve levels from low to high, provides an indication of the \nmodel's ability to conve y information effectively.  \nAccuracy and relevance as further eva luation measures \nrelate directly to the content performance of the model. The \nfive-level rating, which ranges from low to high, takes into \naccount the accuracy of the information provided, its \nrelevance to the prompt, a nd the model's understanding of \nthe content.  \nThe third criterion, overall quality,  and engagement, \ncombines the ratings of expression, logic, accuracy,  and \nrelevance. This approach provides a comprehensive \nassessment of the language model's response, considering  \nboth technical accuracy and the ability to captivate and \nengage the reader.  \nWe improve the depth of our analysis of the chosen \nmodel while demonstrating our commitment to \nunderstanding the language model's performance in real -\nworld scenarios. It conforms to best practices in model \ntesting and v
ing our commitment to \nunderstanding the language model's performance in real -\nworld scenarios. It conforms to best practices in model \ntesting and v alidation and provides valuable insig hts for \nfurther refinement and optimization.  \n2370\nMIPRO 2024/SP
alidation and provides valuable insig hts for \nfurther refinement and optimization.  \n2370\nMIPRO 2024/SP === Page 6 ===
=== Page 6 === C. Results  \nAlthough different configurations give similar answers \nto simpler questions in the provided example, we can see that \nparameter selection can significantly affect the accuracy of \nanswers to more complex questions that require deeper \nunderstanding and broade r context. As a result, the \nconfiguration with the following parameters had the best \nhuman ratings:  \n• Chunk size: 1000  \n• Chunk overlap: 200  \n• Number of retrievals: 5  \n• Maximum number of retrievals: 3  \n• Embedding: all -MiniLM -L6-v2 \nAveraging a total mean rating of 4.13 with an average \nduration per question of 66.55 seconds1.  \nIt is important to note that the context size significantly \naffects the response time. For example, the fastest \nconfiguration with parameters 500, 50, 7, 2, all -MiniLM -L6-\nv2 had
context size significantly \naffects the response time. For example, the fastest \nconfiguration with parameters 500, 50, 7, 2, all -MiniLM -L6-\nv2 had  an average response time of 29.96 seconds and an \noverall human rating of 3.5, while the slowest configuration \nwith parameters 2000, 400, 10, 4, Jina embedding had an \naverage response time of 235.48 seconds per response, with \nan overall human rating of 3.53. Both configurations \nmentioned above used the rerank retriever.  \nIV. CONCLUSION  AND FUTURE WORK  \nIn the described research, a s part of our project, we \nthoroughly investigated the available LLMs, taking into \naccount factors such as model architecture, training data and \nfine-tuning capabilities. This comprehensive understanding \nhelped us select a model that not only understands bu t also \nprovides high -quality, contextualized answers. In addition, \nwe recognized the importance of overcoming challenges \nsuch as hallucinations
u t also \nprovides high -quality, contextualized answers. In addition, \nwe recognized the importance of overcoming challenges \nsuch as hallucinations  and outdated knowledge, which led us \nto integrate the RAG methodology into our proje ct. By \nincorporating knowledge from the external dataset , RAG \nenhanced the accuracy and credibility of our chatbot, \nespecially for knowledge -intensive tasks.  Specifically, we \nutiliz ed available materials  for the course "Introduction to \nProgramming"  containing instructions for installing local \nextensions  in the working environment , instructions for \ncommand prompt environment, instructions for performing \ncourse laboratory exercises, and the overall student guide  for \nthe course . This external dataset consisted of  6 documents \nwith 12.828 words , in total. \nThe process began with model selection, followed by a \nhands -on training phase in which the team fine-tuned  LLMs  \nusing the aforeme
ds , in total. \nThe process began with model selection, followed by a \nhands -on training phase in which the team fine-tuned  LLMs  \nusing the aforeme ntioned official documentation from \n"Introduction to Programming" course.  Following training, \nthe models were evaluated with 20 questions, yielding a \npreliminary assessment that revealed significant \nperformance differences between them. Five models were \nchosen for further testing based on the results of the initial \nevaluation. A fter this step, the evaluation was expanded to \n \n1 All time measurements mentioned in this document were \nobtained using a personal computer equipped with an \nAMD Ryzen7 5800H processor, 16 GB RAM and an include a more diverse set of 92 questions of varying \ncomplexity, all designed to test the models' robustness and \nunderstanding. These questions, some of which contained \nintentional grammatical errors or contexts unrelated to the \ntraining data, were pre
ess and \nunderstanding. These questions, some of which contained \nintentional grammatical errors or contexts unrelated to the \ntraining data, were pre sen ted to the LLMs. A team of 16 \nstudents thoroughly analyzed the responses, using \npredefined metrics to assess the shortlisted models' \nperformance in detail.  \nModels were evaluated in five key areas: fluency, \ncoherence, relevance, context understanding, and overall \nquality, with ratings ranging from 1 to 5 for each criterion. \nTo benchmark, 3 –5 expected answers per question were \nprepared using the LLMs’ training context.  \nBased on comprehensive evaluation criteria, including \nhuman assessment, the Mistral -7B-Openorca model was \nfound to be significantly superior to the others .  \nIn the future, we plan to extend our chatbot project to \nseveral subjects or even to the entire college curriculum. By \nextending the functionality of the chatbot, we hope to \nprovide students with com
t to \nseveral subjects or even to the entire college curriculum. By \nextending the functionality of the chatbot, we hope to \nprovide students with com prehensive support and assistance \nthroughout their studies. This future direction is  consistent \nwith our overall goal of using technology to improve \nlearning outcomes and help students succeed in higher \neducation. The potential success of the chatbot in assisting \nstudents emphasizes its ability to be used in a variety of \nacademic discipli nes, not only in computer engineering. \nUsing the basic framework created through theoretical \nresearch and practical exercises, we can customize and \nrefine the chatbot's capabilities to meet the specific needs \nand requirements of different subjects.  \nFinally, in continuation of our previous research \n[19][20][21], we would like to integrate  the developed AI -\nbased chatbot into an adaptive e -learning intelligent agent \nthat can be used in any
ch \n[19][20][21], we would like to integrate  the developed AI -\nbased chatbot into an adaptive e -learning intelligent agent \nthat can be used in any  learning environment that enables \nremote sensing of students' physiological signals.  The \nintelligent agent evaluates students' engagement and \nlearning curves by analyzing their affective states, \nbehavioral parameters, and psychophysiological features \nusing unobtrusive video surveillance and machine learning. \nThe described system has the po tential to personalize \nlearning and improve pedagogical outcomes, which is \ncritical for creating future e -learning environments.  \nACKNOWLEDGMENT  \nWe sincerely thank all members of the student and \ngraduate teams for their invaluable contributions to this \nproject. Luka Babić, Jure Franjković, Vedran Knežević, Ivan \nLisica, Andrija Merlin, Fran Sipić, Josip Šare, Lora Šušić, \nStela Troskot, in the undergraduate team, and Matej  Božić, \nLovro
