Endocrinology, UL Hospitals Group, Limerick V94 F858, Ireland  \nd School of Medicine , University of Limerick,  Limerick V94 T9PX, Ireland  \n  \nAbstr act  \nThis paper presents the design, architecture, and safety evaluation of an AI chatbot tailored for educational purposes in man aging \nchronic medical conditions, focusing on Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (T2DM). Leveraging conversational agents in health literacy,  \nthe chatbot integrates medically informed information, constrained responses, and response traceability to ensure appropriate ness \nand compliance with protocols. By utilizing ChatGPT with retrieval augmented generation (RAG) and careful prompt \nengineering, the system ensures reliable, traceable, and privacy -conscious interactions. Safety and efficacy testing revealed just \none inappropriate response (5%) in a simulated patient conversation and 15 (75%) fully appropriate responses. This study \nhighlights the potential of AI c
inappropriate response (5%) in a simulated patient conversation and 15 (75%) fully appropriate responses. This study \nhighlights the potential of AI c hatbots in enhancing patient autonomy, reliability, and privacy in accessing medical knowledg e for \nchronic conditions.  \n \n© 2024 The Authors. Published by ELSEVIER B.V.  \nThis is an open access article under the CC BY -NC-ND license  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by -nc-nd/4.0 ) \nPeer-review under responsibility of the scientific committee of the International Society for Research on Internet Interventions \n12th Scientific Meeting  \nKeywords:  conversational agent ; chatbot; ChatGPT ; T2DM ; diabetes; health literacy  \n1. Introduction  \nType 2 di abetes mellitus (T2DM) is a chronic illness char acterized by elevated levels of glucose in the blood \n(hyperglycemia) that results from impaired insulin secretion, insulin resistance or both [1]. The complications of \nuncontrolled T2DM
se in the blood \n(hyperglycemia) that results from impaired insulin secretion, insulin resistance or both [1]. The complications of \nuncontrolled T2DM  can be serious, including microvascular damage to the eyes, kidneys and nerves; and 2 Author name / Procedia Computer Science  00 (2024) 000 –000 \nmacrovascul ar complications such as heart disease, peripher al arterial disease, and stroke  [1]. T2DM is caused by a \ncombination of genetic factors, related insulin secretion, insulin sensitivity, or both  [2]. Environmental factors such \nas obesity, and physical inactivity also play a role [3,4] . Consequently, the prevalence of T2DM has risen in tandem \nwith rising prevalence of the environmental risk factors  [5]; driven by global economics, trade liberalization and \nlifestyle  cha nges [6,7] . As a result, prevalence of T2DM globally has more than tripled from 151 million in 2000 to \n537 million in 2021 [8], with regions undergoing major
] . As a result, prevalence of T2DM globally has more than tripled from 151 million in 2000 to \n537 million in 2021 [8], with regions undergoing major  economic, environmental and lifestyle changes such as Asia, \nAfrica and the Middle East seeing dramatic increases in T2DM incidence [6]. Awareness is a problem, with almost \nhalf of adults with diabetes being unaw are of it [8]. Once aware, education is effective in managing chronic health \nconditions like diabetes, throughout the course of the conditions, when combined with behavior ally oriented \nprograms.  \nConversational agents have been applied in clinical [9] and public health education settings [10], offering \nunconstrained natur al language interaction with the extremely broad knowledge base used for their training. Whilst \nthe training of such models on vast data sets means that these conversational agents can converse meaningfully \nabout many different subjects, the breadth of t
ing of such models on vast data sets means that these conversational agents can converse meaningfully \nabout many different subjects, the breadth of t he ingested information also means that responses can be \nunpredictable and, in some cases, plainly incorrect. As a result, the efficacy and safety of use of such systems, \nparticularly in medical applications, is a concern [11]. \n \n1.1. Health literacy in self-management of chronic disease  \nModifiable risk factors for T2DM, such as obesity, physical inactivity and diet, play a key role at the policy level \nwhere a multilevel systems approach can, for example, help people make healthy choices and reduce the promotion \nof unhealthy choices [7]. Education pl ays a key role in behavior change [12]. Hence, it also plays an important role \nin the self -management of chronic diseases such as T2DM [13,14]  to empower people to eng age in self -care by \ninforming them about their disease and appl
self -management of chronic diseases such as T2DM [13,14]  to empower people to eng age in self -care by \ninforming them about their disease and appl ying this knowledge to help prevent complications of the condition [15]. \nHealth literacy involves the ability to gather and critically evaluate health information in relation to a health \ncondition [16].  Low health literacy represents a significant concern [17]. Patient information is often written in the \nform of booklets provided by medical professionals to guide patients in managing their condition [18]. \nUnfortunately, this approach contributes to poor health literacy due to the limited interactivity and absence of \nopportunities for patients to ask questions [17]. Furthermore, such materials do little to foster skills development and \nthe autonomy required for critical health literacy [14,19] . Health literacy in chronic disease management can be \nimproved by interactivity with medi
the autonomy required for critical health literacy [14,19] . Health literacy in chronic disease management can be \nimproved by interactivity with medi cal literature [16] and improved comprehension by simplifying the material \n[20]. This paper proposes that such interactivity may be provided by AI chatbot based conversational agents \ninformed by medical literature.  \n \n1.2. The role of chatbot conversational agents  \nMore generally, the use of chatbot technology to engage patients interactively by employing artificial intelligence \n(AI) and natur al language processing (NLP), is receiving increased attention [21,22] . The adva nta ges of cha tbot \ntechnology include human -like interactivity with the knowledge base, and autonomy of access to it, potentially \nfulfilling the need for interactive questioning of health management in T2DM. Conversational agents have been \napplied in a variety of clinical and educational settings in healthcare
ive questioning of health management in T2DM. Conversational agents have been \napplied in a variety of clinical and educational settings in healthcare  [9] as well as providing education and support \nin chronic conditions [11] with public acceptance [23]. In particular, generative AI such as ChatGPT have an \nacknowledged role to play in public health education by providing information and answering questions relating to \nhealth promotion, disease prevention, and community health [10]. ChatGPT has been applied in T2DM patient \neducation [21,24] . In an evaluation of answers to a series of questions pertaining to T2DM self management, the \nauthors [21] reported that responses were judged by clinicians as appropriate in 98.5% of cases and were considered \nto be more reliable than search engines. Generative Pretrained Transformers (GPTs) as conversational agents allow \nunconstrained conversational flexibility, autonomy in questioning and hav
s. Generative Pretrained Transformers (GPTs) as conversational agents allow \nunconstrained conversational flexibility, autonomy in questioning and hav e been applied to improving health \nlitera cy [20]. However, there is concern about potential harm from responses that are erroneous [9] or toxic [25],
been applied to improving health \nlitera cy [20]. However, there is concern about potential harm from responses that are erroneous [9] or toxic [25], === Page 4 ===
=== Page 4 === 54 Anthony Kelly et al. / Procedia Computer Science 248 (2024) 52–59\n Aut hor nam e / Procedi a C om puter Science  00 (2024) 000 –000  3 \nand the source of information from ChatGPT is generally unknown. Therefore, ChatGPT does not overcome the \nreliance on unaccredited medical information upon which patients typically rely when engaging in web -ba sed \ninformation seeking [26]. In a cross -sectional study of T2DM management with web search information, Weymann \net a l. [27], found web pages to be insufficient for T2DM management.  \nChatbot models  such as ChatGPT,  a re fine -tuned  Large Language Models  (LLMs ) that respond to prompts and \nuser conversation in a  question and answer format. By responding to prompts, the chat LLM can operate on a \nvariety of tasks without additional fine
user conversation in a  question and answer format. By responding to prompts, the chat LLM can operate on a \nvariety of tasks without additional fine  -tuning.  This makes prompt engineering (designing the most appropriate \nprompt for the task), an important aspect of chatbot design, in what has been called the pre -train, prompt and predict \nparadigm [28].  An example of a typical prompt suitable for this application may be: “- You are a health education \nchatbot for type 2 diabetes (T2DM) patients. - You only discuss the documents provided and information related to \nthem.” , which is designed to direct and constrain the LLM. \nRAG LLMs address the issue of medical credibility  by using specific documents as data on which to answer the \nquery . RAG LLMs  allow attribution to a medical source of information because they process  the documents \nprovided to them  and may be constrained to answer based primarily or solely on the informatio
rce of information because they process  the documents \nprovided to them  and may be constrained to answer based primarily or solely on the informatio n contained within \nthem . RAG LLMs  can be developed to meet ethical design principles that require privacy by default and need to \ninvolve medical professionals [28]. They can be made to attribute the source of the infor mation they provide, \nthrough proper prompt engineering. Therefore, the proposed health literacy intervention should be designed with \nthese principles in mind, providing the proper safeguards over ChatGPT as a n LLM backend. Such capabilities are \nnot readily available to the public today.  \nThis paper describes a chatbot design for education in chronic health conditions that addresses these concerns by: \ni) using medically informed information for medical context; ii) constrained responses for appropriateness and to \ncomply with protocol; iii) response traceability; iv
y informed information for medical context; ii) constrained responses for appropriateness and to \ncomply with protocol; iii) response traceability; iv ) rapid prototyping for iterative, clinical co -design; v) privacy. \nSince evaluation of conversational agents in healthcare for efficacy and safety is limited [11], this paper will \nevaluate the safety of the proposed chatbot when interacted with in the context of T2DM education.  \n \n2. Design and architecture  \nUsers commonly interact with ChatGPT via the interface provided by Open AI. However, this places some \nlimitations on the designer’s ability to control interactions and ensure privacy. Firstly, the ability of users to \ninfluence the prompt is useful for controlling the interaction for the benefit of producing tailored responses, but \nusers can set their own prompts and input prompt attacks have been demonstr ated that can result in toxic responses \n[25]. To control the interaction
users can set their own prompts and input prompt attacks have been demonstr ated that can result in toxic responses \n[25]. To control the interaction  with the LLM the prompt needs to be designed carefully in a way that does not allow \nit to be changed by users. Secondly, the chatbot relies upon RAG that involves interrogating documents with \nChatGPT, with limited recourse to ChatGPT’s general training data. This is in contrast to ChatGPT’s normal \noperation that relies heavily on its general data. Thirdly, only medically informed documents should be used by the \nchatbot. Since by default, Open AI allows users to upload documents, an interface design that does not allow \ndocument upload is required. Fourthly, user privacy cannot be ensured in the default Open AI interface since it \nrequires users to log -in to a personal account. This means a user’s conversations would be associ ated with their \naccount. In a custom interface design, an
s users to log -in to a personal account. This means a user’s conversations would be associ ated with their \naccount. In a custom interface design, an onymity can be achieved by ensuring the interface uses the same \ncredentials for all participants.  These limitations can be overcome by utilizing Open AI’s application programming \ninterface (API), which allows direct interaction with the ChatGPT LLM using the Python programming language. \nThese four requirements were rea lised  in a custom -built interface allowing the user to ask ChatGPT questions \nspecifically about T2DM. To provide flexibility and security, the Python program incorporates the LLM prompt, the \ninterface design, communicates with ChatGPT via a secure API, and is hosted on the Hugging Face Hub cloud \nplatform [29].
porates the LLM prompt, the \ninterface design, communicates with ChatGPT via a secure API, and is hosted on the Hugging Face Hub cloud \nplatform [29]. === Page 5 ===
