=== Page 6 === 56 Anthony Kelly et al. / Procedia Computer Science 248 (2024) 52–59\n Aut hor nam e / Procedi a C om puter Science  00 (2024) 000 –000  5 \nwith a private key managed in Hugging Face’s secure environment. Since Hugging Face does not store any user \ninteractions, other secured APIs allow logging of usage on a database or spreadsheet (e.g. Airtable or Google \nSheets). The report function can be achieved by redirecting users to a collabor ative notice board (e.g. Padlet, \ncommonly used by the academic community). The architecture is illustrated in Fig. 1. \n \n \n3. Evaluation and results  \nTwo documents were selected for the RAG chatbot to access. The first was a patient information booklet [18], \nthe second was a document for healthcare professionals who care for T2DM patients [31]. The patient
The first was a patient information booklet [18], \nthe second was a document for healthcare professionals who care for T2DM patients [31]. The patient  booklet was \nselected to represent a well structured patient centric document that should provide the kinds of answers T2DM \npatients are seeking. The healthcare document was selected for its detailed information, providing relevant  content \nin a way that may be less structured for patients, representing more of a challenge for the chatbot to source from and \noutput patient centric text.  \nFor evaluation, the chatbot was asked questions related to diabetes self -management. Employing a similar \nmethodology to Sng et al. [24] a specialist endocrinologist asked the chatbot a free -flowing series of questions,  \nemulating a typical clinical interaction with a person who has a new diagnosis of type 2 diabetes ( Table 1). The \nanswers were recorded and reviewed by the specialist for accuracy,
teraction with a person who has a new diagnosis of type 2 diabetes ( Table 1). The \nanswers were recorded and reviewed by the specialist for accuracy,  classifying the answers as: a) appropriate (the \nanswer is fully appropriate); b) inappropriate (the answer is not suitable) or; c) partly appropriate (the answer has \naspects that may be inappropriate). The source of the questions was evaluated by a researcher as being a) correctly \nmatched to the source (matched); b) incorrectly matched (unmatched); c) partly matched (meaning the information \nappears in the source but in a different location). The source column lists the status of the evaluation and which \ndocument it was sourced from. For example: Matched (Patient) means the source was correctly matched to the \npatient booklet document. The word “General” in this column means the chatbot stated "this information was \nsourced from my general knowledge.", and “Unsourced” means no source
he word “General” in this column means the chatbot stated "this information was \nsourced from my general knowledge.", and “Unsourced” means no source  was mentioned. As an example of a \ndetailed response, the conversation started with the input:  \n“I have just been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes and I am very worried” . The chatbot responded with: “I \nunderstand that being diagnosed with type 2 diabetes can be overwhelming. It's normal to feel a range of emotions \nsuch as loss, anger, disbelief, and fear. You may feel like life will never be the same again, question why this is \nhappening to you, or worry about the future and potential complications like going blind. It's important to \nremember that with the right support and management, you can lead a healthy and fulfilling life with type 2 \ndiabetes. Some key tips to help manage your diabetes include eating a healthy balanced diet, maintaining a healthy \nweight, incorporating regular p
2 \ndiabetes. Some key tips to help manage your diabetes include eating a healthy balanced diet, maintaining a healthy \nweight, incorporating regular p hysical activity, not smoking, taking your prescribed medication, and monitoring \nyour blood glucose levels as advised by your doctor. Remember, you are not alone in this journey. Seeking support \nfrom healthcare professionals, diabetes educators, and support groups can make a big difference. It's essential to \ntake care of yourself both physically and emotionally. If you have any specific concerns or questions about your \ndiagnosis or managing your diabetes, feel free to ask. It's important to take things one step at a time and prioritize \nyour health and well -being.  \nThis information was sourced from "DIABETES IRELAND – Living Well with Type 2 Diabetes" in the section \n"Recently Diagnosed with Diabetes" and "Top Tips to help Manage Your Diabetes."”.  \n \nTable 1. Free-flow Questions wit
ype 2 Diabetes" in the section \n"Recently Diagnosed with Diabetes" and "Top Tips to help Manage Your Diabetes."”.  \n \nTable 1. Free-flow Questions wit h Evaluation  \nYƵĞƐƚŝŽŶ  ǀĂůƵĂƚŝŽŶ \nŽŶƚĞŶƚ ^ŽƵƌĐĞ\n/ŚĂǀĞũƵƐƚďĞĞŶĚŝĂŐŶŽƐĞĚǁŝƚŚƚǇƉĞϮĚŝĂďĞƚĞƐĂŶĚ/ĂŵǀĞƌǇǁŽƌƌŝĞĚ ƉƉƌŽƉƌŝĂƚĞ  DĂƚĐŚĞĚ;WĂƚŝĞŶƚͿ \ntŝůů/ŐŽďůŝŶĚĨƌŽŵĚŝĂďĞƚĞƐ  ƉƉƌŽƉƌŝĂƚĞ  DĂƚĐŚĞĚ;WĂƚŝĞŶƚͿ \ntŚĂƚĂƌĞƚŚĞĐŚĂŶĐĞƐĚĞǀĞůŽƉŝŶŐĚŝĂďĞƚĞƐĞǇĞĚŝƐĞĂƐĞ  ƉƉƌŽƉƌŝĂƚĞ  DĂƚĐŚĞĚ;WĂƚŝĞŶƚͿ 
