ŶĚĞǆĞƌĐŝƐĞĚ  WĂƌƚůǇƉƉƌŽƉƌŝĂƚĞ DĂƚĐŚĞĚ;WƌŽĨĞƐƐŝŽŶĂůͿ \ntŝůů/ŶĞĞĚŝŶƐƵůŝŶŝĨ /ŚĂǀĞƚǇƉĞϮĚŝĂďĞƚĞƐ  ƉƉƌŽƉƌŝĂƚĞ  DĂƚĐŚĞĚ;WĂƚŝĞŶƚͿ \nŽŵŽƐƚ ƉĞŽƉůĞŶĞĞĚŝŶƐƵůŝŶǁŝƚŚƚǇƉĞϮ ĚŝĂďĞƚĞƐ ƉƉƌŽƉƌŝĂƚĞ  DĂƚĐŚĞĚ;WĂƚŝĞŶƚͿ \nDĂƚĐŚĞĚ;WƌŽĨĞƐƐŝŽŶĂůͿ \n/ƐƚǇƉĞϮĚŝĂďĞƚĞƐƚŚĞŐĞŶĞƚŝĐŽŶĞ  ƉƉƌŽƉƌŝĂƚĞ  DĂƚĐŚĞĚ;WĂƚŝĞŶƚͿ \nĂŶ/ĚƌŝǀĞĂǀĂŶŝĨ /ŚĂǀĞĚŝĂďĞƚĞƐ  WĂƌƚůǇƉƉƌŽƉƌŝĂƚĞ DĂƚĐŚĞĚ;WĂƚŝĞŶƚͿ \n \n \nOut of 20 answers, 19 (95%) were evaluated as partly or fully appropriate, with 1 (5%) being evaluated as \ninappropriate.  Fifteen answers (75%) were evaluated as fully appropriate, whilst 4 answers were evaluated as partly \nappropriate. P artly appropriate answers tended to be too definite, containing some appropriate information, but \nappearing to blame the patient for their illness in a way that failed to communicate the other determinants. For \nexample, the question “Could I have prevented diabetes if I ate better and exercised”  wa
way that failed to communicate the other determinants. For \nexample, the question “Could I have prevented diabetes if I ate better and exercised”  wa s answered as: “Yes, \nmaking healthy lifestyle choices such as eating a healthier diet and exercising regularly can help prevent or delay \nthe onset of type 2 diabetes. These interventions are beneficial in managing blood sugar levels and reducing the risk \nof developing diabetes complications. It's important to note that type 2 diabetes is often linked to lifestyle factors, so \nadopting a healthier lifestyle can have a significant impact on preventing the condition. This information was found \nin the provided documents under the section discussing the management of type 2 diabetes through healthy eating,  \nregular physical activity, and medication.”.  Question 8 to 10, discussing cardiovascular disease in T2DM, caused \nthe chat model to resort to its general knowledge, resulting in an ina
cation.”.  Question 8 to 10, discussing cardiovascular disease in T2DM, caused \nthe chat model to resort to its general knowledge, resulting in an ina ppropriate response in one case and a failure to \nproperly attribute a source in all three cases.  \n \n4. Discussion and Limitations  \nExamination of the partly appropriate responses with respect to the source documents indicates that answers are \nnot as easily sourced from the medical professional’s documents compared to the patient documents. Since the latter \nare written specifically for patients to clearly explain typical concerns, this is understandable. However, this \nobservation shows the importance of selecting suitable documents for the RAG chatbot. The recent availability of \nmore advanced LLMs such as gpt -4o, may improve the answers from the more detailed medical documents. This \nstudy is limited to the models readily available at the time of writing, and requires reassessment w
s from the more detailed medical documents. This \nstudy is limited to the models readily available at the time of writing, and requires reassessment w ith new models as \nthey become available in this fast -moving field.  \nA further limita tion is that the conversa tiona l eva lua tion with a  specia list  is quite narrow in scope . Since the \nresults of this design study have been positive, a more comprehensive evaluation involving more patient queries and \nrecruiting a patient group, would be a promising next step in this work.
positive, a more comprehensive evaluation involving more patient queries and \nrecruiting a patient group, would be a promising next step in this work. === Page 8 ===
=== Page 8 === 58 Anthony Kelly et al. / Procedia Computer Science 248 (2024) 52–59\n Aut hor nam e / Procedi a C om puter Science  00 (2024) 000 –000  7 \n \n5. Conclusion  \nA custom designed chatbot has been demonstrated, building on the capabilities of ChatGPT to help improve \nhealth literacy in the management of T2DM. The chatbot fulfilled the requirements to use medically informed \ninformation, constrain responses and respect privacy, in a flexible development environment.  \nWhen evaluated on a specialist led simulated patient conversation, 75% of the answers (15) were evaluated to be \nfully appropriate and 20% (5) as partially appropriate. Only one answer (5%) was evaluated as inappropriate.  \nThis evaluation shows the fea sibility  of the intervention. The few inappropriate and partly appropriate res
r (5%) was evaluated as inappropriate.  \nThis evaluation shows the fea sibility  of the intervention. The few inappropriate and partly appropriate res ponses \nwere due to the RAG chatbot straying away from the contents of the documents sourced by it. Forcing the chatbot to \nanswer exclusively from the source documents could be quite limiting in the breadth of health literacy patients need.  \nFuture work may  trial this approach with patients and  examine other methods to ensure the chatbot balances the \nneeds of medical rigor and patient autonomy in health literacy.  \n \nReferences  \n[1] Welch A, Vella A. What is type 2 diabetes? Medicine (United Kingdom) 2022;50:625 –31. \nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpmed.2022.07.003.  \n[2] Malone JI, Hansen BC. Does obesity cause type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM)? Or is it the opposite? Pediatr Diabetes 2019;20:5 –9. \nhttps://doi.org/10.1111/PEDI.12787.  \n[3] Reed J, Bain S, Kanamarlapudi V. A review of cur
