=== Page 1 === Vol.:(0123456789)Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:7841–7862\nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/s10639-024-13129-5\nDeveloping a computer-based tutor utilizing Generative \nArtificial Intelligence (GAI) and Retrieval-Augmented \nGeneration (RAG)\nYoungjin Lee1 \nReceived: 19 April 2024 / Accepted: 18 October 2024 / Published online: 4 November 2024 \n© The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature \n2024\nAbstract\nThis study investigates the development and evaluation of a Retrieval-Augmented \nGeneration (RAG)-based statistics tutor designed to assist students with quantita-\ntive analysis methods. The RAG approach was employed to address the well-doc-\numented issue of hallucination in Large Language Models (LLMs). A computer \ntutor was
methods. The RAG approach was employed to address the well-doc-\numented issue of hallucination in Large Language Models (LLMs). A computer \ntutor was  developed that utilizes ChatGPT to understand student questions in natu-\nral language and retrieves relevant information from validated course notes to gen-\nerate responses. The tutor was evaluated using twenty questions curated from the \ncourse notes. Two instructors who taught the course using the course notes assessed \nthe quality of the tutor’s response using a pre-defined rubric. The evaluation results \nindicate that the RAG-based tutor was able to provide correct and relevant answers \nto all twenty questions and to generate R code snippets upon request. This study \nhighlights the potential of RAG-based tutors to provide accurate and personalized \nlearning experiences for students while mitigating the risk of providing fabricated \ninformation often associated with LLMs.\nKeywords Compu
sonalized \nlearning experiences for students while mitigating the risk of providing fabricated \ninformation often associated with LLMs.\nKeywords Compu ter-based tutor · Generative Artificial Intelligence (GAI) · \nRetrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) · Statistics education\n1 Introduction\nArtificial Intelligence (AI) has been heralded as a transformative force in educa-\ntion, especially for its potential to provide personalized learning experiences tai-\nlored to the needs of individual students (Holmes et al., 2019; Luckin et al., 2016). \nTraditional AI applications, such as Intelligent Tutoring System (ITS), have shown \n * Youngjin Lee \n Youngjin.Lee@unt.edu\n1 Department of Learning Technologies, College of Information, University of North Texas, 3940 \nN. Elm St. Suite G157, 76207 Denton, TX, USA\nContent courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply. Rights reserved.
niversity of North Texas, 3940 \nN. Elm St. Suite G157, 76207 Denton, TX, USA\nContent courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply. Rights reserved. === Page 2 ===
=== Page 2 === 7842 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:7841–7862\nprogress in adapting instructional content, pacing and feedback to individual learner \n(VanLehn, 2011; Kulik & Fletcher, 2016). These applications use computational \nalgorithms to assess the performance of learners and provide targeted interventions \nand resources that can improve learning outcomes (Pane et al., 2014).\nAs the field of AI continues to evolve, the advent of Generative AI (GAI), such as \nChatGPT, has further enhanced the feasibility of providing truly personalized edu-\ncation. GAI is built on Large Language Models (LLMs) trained on vast amounts of \ntext data available on the Internet, enabling them to generate human-like text based \non the input they receive. The underlying LLMs are fine-tuned through advanced \nd
le on the Internet, enabling them to generate human-like text based \non the input they receive. The underlying LLMs are fine-tuned through advanced \nd eep-learning-based natural language processing techniques to improve perfor -\nmance in specific domains or tasks. GAI can generate contents that are dynamically \ntailored to each student’s need, allowing for more nuanced and adaptive approaches \nto providing personalized learning experiences, where learning materials are created \nin response to student interactions. For instance, GAI can be employed to develop \ninteractive tutorials that cater to students’ specific questions and pace of learning, \nhelping them to walk through complex concepts or problem-solving steps. This \napproach can potentially improve student learning by providing more personalized \nand adaptive learning experience.\nAs noted above, education researchers have been developing ITS to provide per -\nsonalized and adaptive
ersonalized \nand adaptive learning experience.\nAs noted above, education researchers have been developing ITS to provide per -\nsonalized and adaptive  learning experience. Although ITS and GAI-based tutoring \nsystem share the common goal of providing customized learning experience to stu-\ndents, there exist a significant difference. ITS, such as Carnegie Mellon University’s \nCognitive Tutor (Koedinger et al., 1997), requires a rule-based cognitive model of \ntarget knowledge (e.g., how to add two fractions with different denominators) to \nunderstand student learning patterns and deliver tailored instructional supports. As \na result, ITS typically focuses on structured subjects like mathematics and it may \nnot be applicable to areas in which it is not easy to extract a set of explicit rules \ncapturing important concepts and their relationships. On the other hand, GAI-based \ntutoring systems leverage natural language processing to engage in
capturing important concepts and their relationships. On the other hand, GAI-based \ntutoring systems leverage natural language processing to engage in  dynamic, con-\nversation-based interactions with students, offering assistance across a much wider \narray of knowledge domains.\nHowever, GAI-based tutoring systems have its own weaknesses. One significant \nweakness of GAI-based tutoring systems is tied to its knowledge cutoff date. The \nunderlying GAI’s training data only includes information available up to a spe-\ncific date. Therefore, this temporal limitation can result in outdated or inaccurate \nresponses from GAI-based tutoring systems. A more serious issue is that since the \nunderlying GAI model “generates” its responses from language patterns learned \nfrom vast training datasets, it is susceptible to producing contents that, while sound \nplausible, might be factually incorrect or entirely fabricated, which is often called \n“hallucin
susceptible to producing contents that, while sound \nplausible, might be factually incorrect or entirely fabricated, which is often called \n“hallucin ation” (Farquhar et  al., 2024; Weise & Metz, 2023; Xu et  al., 2024). In \neducational settings, where the accuracy of information is critical, the risk of hal-\nlucination is concerning.\nIn order to address the issue of hallucination in GAI, researchers have developed \nseveral approaches. First, since LLMs that power GAI tools acquire most of their \nknowledge during pretraining (Zhou et  al., 2024), the hallucination problem can be \nmitigated by excluding unreliable, defective and noisy data that cannot be verified \nContent courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply. Rights reserved.
d by excluding unreliable, defective and noisy data that cannot be verified \nContent courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply. Rights reserved. === Page 3 ===
=== Page 3 === 7843\n Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:7841–7862 \nwhile pretraining the model (Penedo et  al., 2023; Touvron et  al., 2023). Similarly, \nhallucinations can be reduced during the fine-tuning stage by using carefully curated \nprompts and responses (Zhou et  al., 2024). Another approach to mitigating the hal-\nlucination problem is to employ reinforcement learning to distinguish desirable out-\nputs from undesirable ones during the model building process (Lightman et al., 2023). \nAlthough these approaches have proven effective in mitigating hallucinations in LLMs, \nthey require modifications to the internal structure of the LLM model (e.g., weights and \nbiases of the neural network model), making it accessible only to computer scientists \ncapable of building natural language mod
el (e.g., weights and \nbiases of the neural network model), making it accessible only to computer scientists \ncapable of building natural language mod els using deep learning.\nAnother approach to addressing these issues is Retrieval-Augmented Generation \n(RAG) (Lewis et al., 2020). Unlike conventional GAI models, which rely solely on \nlearned patterns during training to generate responses, RAG integrates a retrieval \nmechanism to pull relevant information from predefined, validated sources. RAG \naddresses the problem of outdated knowledge by allowing the underlying LLM to \nincorporate up-to-date information, mitigating the impact of a static knowledge \nbase. Moreover, RAG helps alleviate the hallucination problem by generating \nresponses in the context of information retrieved from a validated knowledge base, \nenabling the model to verify its output (Extance, 2023). While fine-tuning updates \nthe internal parameters of an LLM on a specif
ted knowledge base, \nenabling the model to verify its output (Extance, 2023). While fine-tuning updates \nthe internal parameters of an LLM on a specif ic dataset to adapt the model to a par -\nticular task or domain, RAG tries to improve the LLM’s performance by incorporat-\ning external knowledge sources without modifying the model’s internal parameters \n(Balaguer et al., 2024). Since RAG does not involve changing the LLM’s parame-\nters, it requires much less computational effort and is much more accessible to those \nwithout a background in natural language processing and deep learning.\nThis exploratory study aims to answer the following research question: How \neffective is a GAI-based tutor employing RAG in providing answers to questions in \nquantitative research methods? To address this research question, a GAI-based sta-\ntistics tutor was first developed using ChatGPT API (OpenAI, 2024a), LlamaIndex \n(LlamaIndex, 2024) and Streamlit (S
search question, a GAI-based sta-\ntistics tutor was first developed using ChatGPT API (OpenAI, 2024a), LlamaIndex \n(LlamaIndex, 2024) and Streamlit (S treamlit, 2024). The quality of the tutor’s expla-\nnations to free-form questions was then evaluated in terms of weather they included \nincorrect or excessive information. The rest of the paper is organized as follows. The \nliterature review section summarizes previous studies on GenAI in education, hallu-\ncinations in GAI and RAG. The methods section explains what RAG is, how it was \nused in developing a computer tutor that can answer questions about statistical tests, \nand how the responses of this tutor were evaluated. The result section summarizes \nthe correctness and excessiveness of the tutor’s responses to evaluation questions. \nFinally, the paper concludes with discussions of implications and limitations of the \nstudy.\n2  Literature review\n2.1  GAI in education\nSince the introducti
y, the paper concludes with discussions of implications and limitations of the \nstudy.\n2  Literature review\n2.1  GAI in education\nSince the introducti on of ChatGPT 3.5, education researchers’ interest in GAI has \nsurged as they observed its potential to revolutionize how we teach and learn new \nContent courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply. Rights reserved.
as they observed its potential to revolutionize how we teach and learn new \nContent courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply. Rights reserved. === Page 4 ===
=== Page 4 === 7844 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:7841–7862\nknowledge. Despite this growing enthusiasm, research on GAI in education is \nstill in its infancy. Most studies are largely conceptual or theoretical, focusing on \nhow GAI might be used or discussing the pros and cons of its application in vari-\nous educational contexts. For example, Lim et al. (2023) described the paradoxes \nof GAI, such as being both a friend and foe, capable yet dependent, accessible yet \nrestrictive, and popular and banned, and explained the implications of incorporat-\ning GAI in education. Lodge et al. (2023) compared GenAI tools to calculators in \nterms of whether the tool is used individually or collaboratively, and whether the \ntool offloads or extends the cognition of learners. Tan et al. (2023) conce
whether the tool is used individually or collaboratively, and whether the \ntool offloads or extends the cognition of learners. Tan et al. (2023) conce ptualized \nhow GenAI can be used to support collaborative learning. They articulated chal-\nlenges and approaches for the sustainable, reliable, ethical and responsible use of \nGenAI in collaborative learning environment from the perspective of students, \nteachers, administrators and AI developers. Salah et al. (2023) examined the benefits \nand limitations of using ChatGPT in social psychology research, offering recom-\nmendations for its practical and responsible use. Hashmi and Bal (2024) explored \nthe relationship between transparency and responsibility in the use of GenAI. They \nalso discussed how higher education institutions could engage with GenAI to better \nprepare students for a future society where the use of GenAI would be more preva-\nlent. Alasadi and Baiz (2023) discussed the im
with GenAI to better \nprepare students for a future society where the use of GenAI would be more preva-\nlent. Alasadi and Baiz (2023) discussed the im pact of implementing AI in chemistry \neducation in terms of advancing educational goals, ethical considerations, profes-\nsional activities, equity and accessibility.\nAnother popular line of research explores the perceptions of students, teachers \nand administrators regarding the use of GAI in education. Chen et al. (2023) inter -\nviewed 10 high school students who used ChatGPT to examine how it supported \ntheir knowledge building efforts. Lee et al. (2024) surveyed 30 teaching staff and \ninterviewed 8 of them regarding the impact of AI on higher education. Their the-\nmatic analysis revealed a lack of homogeneous sentiment about using AI, signifi-\ncant ambiguity regarding best practices for utilizing advanced technology tools, and \nconcerns around academic integrity. Chan and Hu (2023) inve
ifi-\ncant ambiguity regarding best practices for utilizing advanced technology tools, and \nconcerns around academic integrity. Chan and Hu (2023) inve stigated students’ per -\nception of GenAI using a survey administered to 339 undergraduate and graduate \nstudents. They found that students generally had a positive attitude towards GenAI \nin teaching and learning, but also raised concerns about accuracy, privacy, ethical \nissues and the impact on personal development, career prospects and societal val-\nues. Kaplan-Rakowski and Grotewold (2023) explored teachers’ perspectives on \nGAI and its potential implementation in education, focusing on its use, integra-\ntion, potential and concerns. They found that teachers in general expressed positive \nattitudes towards GAI regardless of their teaching style. Moorhouse et  al. (2023) \nexamined how the world’s 50 top-ranking higher education institutions developed \nor modified their assessment guide
g style. Moorhouse et  al. (2023) \nexamined how the world’s 50 top-ranking higher education institutions developed \nor modified their assessment guide lines in response to emerging GAI. Smolansky \net al. (2023) investigated the perspectives of educators and students on the impact of \nGenAI on assessments in higher education. They found that educators prefer adapted \nassessment incorporating GenAI and encouraging critical thinking, while students’ \nresponses were mixed due to concerns about a loss of creativity. While these discus-\nsions are crucial for understanding the broader implications of incorporating GIA in \neducation, there is very little research that reports on the actual outcomes of utiliz-\ning GAI in educational contexts.\nContent courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply. Rights reserved.
t reports on the actual outcomes of utiliz-\ning GAI in educational contexts.\nContent courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply. Rights reserved. === Page 5 ===
=== Page 5 === 7845\n Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:7841–7862 \n2.2  Hallucinations in generative AI\nGenerative Artificial Intelligence (GAI), while exhibiting remarkable capabilities, \nis known to produce responses containing false or misleading information (Ji et al., \n2023). This phenomenon is commonly referred as hallucination, and it has been an \nimportant focus of research due to its implications in credibility of GAI-generated \noutputs. Much of research evaluating the credibility of text generated by GAI has \ncentered on assessing the correctness of bibliographies generated by Large Lan-\nguage Models (LLMs). Sharun et al. (2023) utilized ChatGPT to compose 20 essays \non stem cell research. The examination of 86 unique journal references cited in these \nessays revealed that 13 (15.
ized ChatGPT to compose 20 essays \non stem cell research. The examination of 86 unique journal references cited in these \nessays revealed that 13 (15. 12%) were fabricated, and 8 (9.30%) were erroneous ref-\nerences. Similarly, McGowan et  al. (2023) focused on the accuracy of references \ngenerated by ChatGPT and Bard in a literature review on Psychiatry research. Out \nof the 35 references examined, only two were found to be real, with the majority \nwere patchworks of citations from existing papers, raising concerns about mod-\nels’ ability to produce reliable academic content. Zuccon et al. (2023) investigated \nwhether ChatGPT can provide evidence to supports its answers. Despite yielding \ncorrect or partially correct answers 51% of the time, the references suggested by \nChatGPT existed only 14% of the time. Furthermore, even existing references often \nfailed to support the claims attributed by ChatGPT. Walters and Wilder (2023) pro-\nduce
14% of the time. Furthermore, even existing references often \nfailed to support the claims attributed by ChatGPT. Walters and Wilder (2023) pro-\nduce d short literature reviews on various topics using ChatGPT 3.5 and ChatGPT \n4. When they analyzed 636 bibliographic citations included in the literature review, \nthey found that 55% of ChatGPT 3.5 citations and 18% of ChatCPT 4 citations were \nfabricated. Additionally, a significant portion of non-fabricated citations contained \nsubstantive errors. Maynez et al. (2020) conducted a large-scale human evaluation \nof neural abstractive summarization systems, including Transformer-based Gener -\nated Pre-Trained (GPT) language models. Across various systems, including GPT \nmodels, substantial amounts of hallucinated content were identified in generative \nsummaries.\nWhile most of previous studies investigating hallucination in AGI have focused \non the bibliographies of GAI-generated text, there i
ative \nsummaries.\nWhile most of previous studies investigating hallucination in AGI have focused \non the bibliographies of GAI-generated text, there i s a growing number of studies \nexamining different sources of text. For instance, Rawte et al. (2023) used a data set \nof New York Times news event tweets to investigate how linguistic factors, such as \nreadability (Flesch, 1948), formality (Heylighen & Dewaele, 1999) and concreteness \n(Brysbaertet al., 2014), influence the occurrence of hallucinations. Their analysis \nshowed that more complex, less formal, and more abstract prompts resulted in more \nhallucinations. Varshney et al. (2023) examined the occurrence of hallucinations in \narticles generated by ChatGPT 3.5 across various categories (e.g., sports, music, \npolitics, film and TV, history, services, science, art, literature and others). When \nthey manually checked the correctness of the generated text using Web searches, \nthey found
ory, services, science, art, literature and others). When \nthey manually checked the correctness of the generated text using Web searches, \nthey found  that about half of the generated text contained incorrect information. \nLadhak et al. (2023) used a Wikipedia Name-Nationality dataset (“Lists of people \nby nationality”, 2024) to investigate the name-nationality hallucinations in LLM-\ngenerated summary text. They found that the hallucination rate was significantly \nhigher for Asian nationalities (about 33%) compared to those from countries in \nAmerica (less than 5%). McIntosh et al. (2024) evaluated the responses from seveval \nContent courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply. Rights reserved.
(less than 5%). McIntosh et al. (2024) evaluated the responses from seveval \nContent courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply. Rights reserved. === Page 6 ===
=== Page 6 === 7846 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:7841–7862\nLLMs, including ChatGPT 3.5, ChatGPT 4, Bard, Perplexity AI and TruthGPT, to \nquestions in 7 categories (religious disputes, political disagreements, ethical dilem-\nmas, pseudoscience, social norm expectations, health misinformation and brain teas-\ners). They found that topics lacking a clear scientific consensus and the brain teasers \nwere more susceptible to hallucinations.\n2.3  Addressing hallucinations with Retrieval‑Augmented Generation (RAG)\nRecently, researchers have begun exploring how RAG can mitigate GAI’s hallu-\ncination problem. Zakka et al. (2024) demonstrated that an LLM augmented with \nretrieval capabilities outperformed standard LLMs in the medical field, as evaluated \nby experts across multiple specialties. Si
n LLM augmented with \nretrieval capabilities outperformed standard LLMs in the medical field, as evaluated \nby experts across multiple specialties. Si milarly, Ge et al. (2023) showcased the suc-\ncessful integration of protected health information into an LLM tailored for liver \ndisease inquiries. Lála et  al. (2023) highlighted the advantages of RAG, showing \nimproved performance in answering questions over scientific literature compared \nto traditional LLMs. Saba et al. (2024) emphasized the benefits of RAG, including \nminimal training requirements, diversity in responses, and avoidance of hallucina-\ntion issues common in LLMs. Shuster et al. (2021) supports the potential of RAG in \nmitigating hallucination problems in open-domain question answering, while Lewis \net al. (2020) presents a RAG model that outperforms other NLP models on knowl-\nedge-intensive tasks, leveraging both pre-trained seq2seq models and dense vector \nindices. Fina
RAG model that outperforms other NLP models on knowl-\nedge-intensive tasks, leveraging both pre-trained seq2seq models and dense vector \nindices. Fina lly, Wang et al. (2024) demonstrated the effectiveness of RAG in syn-\nthesizing information from multiple sources for personalized response generation. \nOverall, these studies collectively underscore the value of RAG in enhancing the \ncapabilities and reliabilities of language models across diverse applications.\n3  Methods\n3.1  RAG‑Based statistics Tutor\nThis study aims to develop and evaluate a computer tutor that communicate with \nstudents in natural language when they need help on quantitative research methods. \nTo enable the tutor to understand students’ questions, the tutor developed in this \nstudy utilized ChatGPT API (OpenAI, 2024a). Additionally, to prevent the tutor \nfrom providing incorrect information, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) \n(Lewis et al., 2020) was employed in de
dditionally, to prevent the tutor \nfrom providing incorrect information, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) \n(Lewis et al., 2020) was employed in de veloping the tutor.\nFigure  1 depicts how the computer tutor is utilizing RAG in answering a ques-\ntion. First, the text in the course notes, which covers t-test and ANOVA, are bro-\nken into smaller chunks to overcome ChatGPT’s limitation on the size of contextual \ninformation (Chunks). These smaller chunks are then converted into a sequence of \nnumeric values called embedding to help the tutor locate most relevant chunks to \na user’s question (Embedded Chunks). The statistics tutor developed in this study \nutilized OpenAI’s “text-embedding-ada-003” embedding model which converts \na given text into a series of 3,071 numerical values (OpenAI, 2024b). Since text \nContent courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply. Rights reserved.
en text into a series of 3,071 numerical values (OpenAI, 2024b). Since text \nContent courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply. Rights reserved. === Page 7 ===
=== Page 7 === 7847\n Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:7841–7862 \nembeddings can capture the semantic meaning of words and phrases in documents, \nit allows us to find relevant information based on “semantic similarity” rather than \nexact word matching (Jurafsky & Martin, 2024). The embedded smaller chunks are \nstored in a vector database for searching. The statistics tutor developed in this study \nused Chroma (Chroma, 2024), an open-source vector database, to store embedded \ntext chunks and perform semantic search. When a user asks a question, the question \nis converted into a sequence of numeric values using the same embedding model \n(Embedded Question). The embedded user question is used to select the most rel-\nevant chunks from the Chroma vector database (Retrieve). The retrieved chunks
ed Question). The embedded user question is used to select the most rel-\nevant chunks from the Chroma vector database (Retrieve). The retrieved chunks  \nand the user’s question are then sent to ChatGPT 3.5 (Augment) with an instruc-\ntion that the retrieved chunks should be used as context for answering the user’s \nquestion (Generate). The statistics tutor developed in this study utilized an open-\nsource Python library called LlamaIndex (LlamaIndex, 2024) to implement a RAG \napproach. Specifically, it used LlamaIndex to generate a question based on the rel-\nevant chunks retrieved from the Chroma vector database and the history of tutor-\nuser conversations. It then requested ChatGPT 3.5 to answer the question based on \nthe relevant chunks and conversation history. Since ChatGPT generates its response \nfrom the provided the context (relevant chunks and conversation history), it is less \nlikely to produce a fabricated answer. Also, when the
response \nfrom the provided the context (relevant chunks and conversation history), it is less \nlikely to produce a fabricated answer. Also, when the  tutor asked ChatGPT-3.5 to \nanswer questions, it set the “temperature” parameter of ChatGPT, which controls the \nlevel of randomness in ChatGPT’s responses, to zero to make its responses as deter -\nministic as possible. As a result, the tutor received very similar answers, though not \nexactly same, since the response is still “generated” by LLM, when the same ques-\ntion was asked repeatedly.\nStreamlit (Streamlit, 2024), an open source Python library that makes it easy \nto create Web applications for machine learning and data science, was used to \nShow me an R co de \nperforming an \nindependent samples \nt-test. Provide a \nconcise explanation \nabout your R co de.User question\nCourse notes\nChun ks\n[0.3, 0.4, 0.1, …]\n[0.7, 1.4, 2.1, …]\n[1.2, 0.3, 1.2, …]\n…Embedded  Chun ksEmbedding \nModelVector
