fies how the variables in the data frame should be mapped to \nthe aesthetics of the plot. In the given context, `x = gear` and `y = mpg` are specified  to map the \n`gear` variable to the x-axis and the `mpg` variable to the y-axis.\n  - `fill`: This argument specifies the fill color of the boxes in the plot. By default, the boxes are filled \nwith a solid color. You can specify a specific color or use a variable from the data frame to map \nthe fill color.\n  - `color`: This argument specifies the color of the outlines of the boxes and whiskers. By default, \nthe outlines are black. You can specify a specific color or use a variable from the data frame to \nmap the color.\n  - `size`: This argument specifies the size of the outlines of the boxes and whiskers. By default, the \nsize is 0.5. You can specify a different size value to make the outlines thicker or thinner.\n  - `alpha`: This argument specifies the transparency of the boxes and whisker
specify a different size value to make the outlines thicker or thinner.\n  - `alpha`: This argument specifies the transparency of the boxes and whisker s. By default, the \ntransparency is 1 (fully opaque). You can specify a value between 0 and 1 to make the boxes and \nwhiskers more transparent.\nThese are just a few of the arguments that can be used with `geom_boxplot()`. There are many more \noptions available to customize the appearance of the plot, such as adding labels, changing axis \ntitles, adjusting the position of the boxes, and more. You can refer to the ggplot2 documentation \nfor more details on the available options.\nQ13 To compute descriptive statistics (mean, median, and standard deviation) on groups for ANOVA \nusing R, you can use the `group_by()` and `summarize()` functions from the `dplyr` package. \nFirst, use the `group_by()` function to specify the grouping variable. Then, use the `summarize()` \nfunction to calculate the d
the `dplyr` package. \nFirst, use the `group_by()` function to specify the grouping variable. Then, use the `summarize()` \nfunction to calculate the d esired statistics for each group. For example, to compute the mean, \nmedian, and standard deviation of a variable `x` for each group defined by a factor variable \n`group`, you can use the following code:\n```{r}\ndf %>%\ngroup_by(group) %>%\nsummarize(mean = mean(x),\n  median = median(x),\n  sd = sd(x))\n```\nReplace `df` with the name of your data frame, `group` with the name of your factor variable, and \n`x` with the name of the variable for which you want to compute the statistics. The resulting data \nframe will contain the mean, median, and standard deviation for each group.\nQ14 The alternative hypothesis in ANOVA is that the mean values in each group are not the same.\nContent courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply. Rights reserved.
