=== Page 8 === Embodied AI-Guided Interactive Digital Teachers for Education SA Educator’s Forum ’24, December 03–06, 2024, Tokyo, Japan\nVirtual Reality (VR) is an intermediate stage \nnecessary for the development of the metaverse. \nIt serves as a technical foundation for the \nmetaverse, allowing users to interact with each \nother in a shared digital environment.\nWhat is user interactivity in metaverse \ntechnology ?\nIn the context of metaverse technology, user \ninteractivity refers to the ways in which users \ninteract with virtual environments and objects \nwithin them, such as input techniques and \nauthoring systems for content creation.\nA digital twin is a digital clone of a physical entity \nor system that has high integrity and \nconsciousness, interacting with the physical world. \nIt can provide servic
digital clone of a physical entity \nor system that has high integrity and \nconsciousness, interacting with the physical world. \nIt can provide servic es such as classification, \nrecognition, prediction, and determination for its \nphysical entities.What is digital twin ?\nWhat are the ethical issues in \nmetaverse ?\nSome of the ethical issues in metaverse include \npropagating offensive messages (e.g., race), avatar -\ninduced user behaviors, user privacy, fairness, and \nconnections with physical worlds. Additionally, there \nare concerns about regulating the metaverse and \ngovernance possibilities, such as censorship and \nblocking access to certain virtual objects and \nenvironments.\nWhat is Social VR ?\nSocial VR is a type of virtual reality experience that \nallows multiple users to interact with each other in \na shared virtual space. It enables social \ncommunication and interaction, where forming \nsocial relationships and communities is poss
h each other in \na shared virtual space. It enables social \ncommunication and interaction, where forming \nsocial relationships and communities is poss ible.\nI want to learn more about immersive art \ntechnology.\nImmersive art technologies refer to the use of \nvirtual elements like images, sound, and other \ninteractive digital components to create an \nartificial, interactive environment that simulates \na sense of physical presence. This can include \nvirtual reality (VR) experience, augmented reality, \nand mix reality environments. These \ntechnologies aim to provide users with a vivid \nillusion of reality, making them feel immersed in \nthe digital world.\nWhat are the differences between VR and \nAR?\nThe main difference between Virtual Reality (VR) and \nAugmented Reality (AR) is that VR creates a \ncompletely artificial environment, whereas AR \nenhances the physical world by overlaying virtual \ninformation onto it. In other words, VR immerses
a \ncompletely artificial environment, whereas AR \nenhances the physical world by overlaying virtual \ninformation onto it. In other words, VR immerses  \nusers in a fully virtual world, while AR blends virtual \nobjects with real -world surroundings.\nWhat are the security problems in \nmetaverse?\nOne of the major concerns in the metaverse is the \nprivacy risk, as it will collect more user data than \never, including biometrics such as audio and iris \nrecognition. If compromised, these biometrics \ncannot be revoked or reset like passwords can.\nWhat is virtual reality in \nmetaverse ?\nFigure 5: Examples of conversation with our chatbot on metaverse knowledge.\nuser studies to collect feedback from users to help us improve the\nsystem. Furthermore, we aim to support more languages beyond\nEnglish leveraging AI technology, making the system accessible\nto audiences around the world. We also plan to support diverse\ntypes of interactions, such as voice
aging AI technology, making the system accessible\nto audiences around the world. We also plan to support diverse\ntypes of interactions, such as voice  commands, gestures, and aug-\nmented reality interfaces, to provide a richer and more engaging\nuser experience.\nAcknowledgments\nThis research is supported in part by the Guangzhou Municipal\nNansha District Science and Technology Bureau under Contract\nNo.2022ZD012.References\nJosh Achiam, Steven Adler, Sandhini Agarwal, Lama Ahmad, Ilge Akkaya, Floren-\ncia Leoni Aleman, Diogo Almeida, Janko Altenschmidt, Sam Altman, Shyamal\nAnadkat, et al .2023. Gpt-4 technical report. arXiv preprint arXiv:2303.08774 (2023).\nSaikiran Anasingaraju, Meng-Lin Wu, Nicoletta Adamo-Villani, Voicu Popescu, Su-\nsan Wagner Cook, Mitchell Nathan, and Martha Alibali. 2016. Digital learning\nactivities delivered by eloquent instructor avatars: scaling with problem instance.\nInSiggraph asia 2016 symposium on education . 1–
