llustrated in Fig. 2 (Bottom Left).\n•RQ2: A 80%agreement was reached by all cohort faculty members in the potential of our RAG system as a virtual\ntea ching assistant for computer science higher education as illustrated in Fig. 2 (Bottom Right).\nA majority agreement allows us to see the potential of the retrieval augmented generation technique using state-of-\nthe-art LLMs in higher-educational settings. There seems to be better acceptance owing to higher potential as virtual\nTeaching Assistant over Teaching Aid . The feedback and opinions reported in Section 4.3 allow us to understand the\nimprovements that would allow for full-scale deployment and acceptance with improved ratings.\n4.3 Faculty Perspectives\nTo delve into reasoning for rating and understanding the potential and challenges of RAG systems in computer science\nhigher education, we investigate the reported feedback on the same. The feedback directly provides information on the\ni
AG systems in computer science\nhigher education, we investigate the reported feedback on the same. The feedback directly provides information on the\ni mprovements needed for large-scale deployment and implementation, with improved faculty ratings. The feedback\nfrom each of the cohort study members was filtered and presented to maintain relevance to the two tasks and can be\nsummarized as follows:\n•Faculty 1: The RAG system can help answer student’s questions when trying to complete homework assignments\nbut would benefit from multiple sources of information such as multiple instructor slides and multiple textbooks.\nFurthermore, it needs a mechanism for reporting so that faculty can check usage/correctness of answers provided\nto students, and needs a mechanism for the interface so that students can email bot’s responses to faculty\nmembers if they feel that they are being given incorrect information.\n•Faculty 2: While the systems can help st
s can email bot’s responses to faculty\nmembers if they feel that they are being given incorrect information.\n•Faculty 2: While the systems can help st udents as a Teaching Assistant to some extent, it needs better capabilities\nto solve simple problems based on equations that were discussed in the class.\n•Faculty 3: The system can answer basic questions and would benefit from integrating general knowledge. The\nfaculty members also mentioned that they would not use this bot in this form in their courses.\n•Faculty 4: No, it still needs improvement and should be trained with more variations of prompts. For now, it is\nonly text-based; it needs to be compatible with non-text files.\n•Faculty 5: The system have capability of answering questions for students and generating basic question answers\nas quizlets for students. However, the model needs the context of different types of questions, such as MCQs\nand short answers, to generate questions or
quizlets for students. However, the model needs the context of different types of questions, such as MCQs\nand short answers, to generate questions or  assignments. Moreover, the model does not perform well on the\ninformation from the images.\nManuscript Accepted at ACM SIGITE 2024
