ch interest\nContact Information === Slide 3 === Embracing the digital education era and seizing opportunities through innovative means === Slide 4 === Outline\n[使用此空间放置最能捕捉主要观点 #1 的图像]\nIntroduction\nBackground\nLiterature Review\nLocal Deployment of RAG Systems and Personalized Learning\nAdvantages and Architecture of Locally Deployed RAG Systems\nTeaching Case\nFuture outlook and opportunities\n    Q&A === Slide 5 === Introduction\nUnderstanding Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)\nRetrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)s potential in education\n Aim and Scope of the RAG Application [使用此空间放置最能捕捉主要观点 #2 的图像] === Slide 6 ===
tion (RAG)\nRetrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)s potential in education\n Aim and Scope of the RAG Application [使用此空间放置最能捕捉主要观点 #2 的图像] === Slide 6 === Background\nWhat is the problem for LLM？\nLimited Context Windows: Inability to process and understand large amounts of context\nSpecialized Content Generation: Difficulty in generating accurate and specialized content\nGeneralized Expressions: Tendency to produce generic and non-specific responses\nHallucinations: Interference of false or irrelevant information in generated content\nGenerative AI, particularly Large Language Models (LLMs), has emerged as a transformative force in education, providing numerous opportunities to enhance interaction and engagement in the learning process.\nLLMs are designed to provide personalized learning experiences by adapting content and explanations to individual student needs, fostering a more engaging and effective learning environment.\nHowever, the implement
by adapting content and explanations to individual student needs, fostering a more engaging and effective learning environment.\nHowever, the implement ation of generative AI in education requires careful consideration of several key factors, including ethical implications, privacy, and the need for human oversight to ensure the accuracy and appropriateness of content.
several key factors, including ethical implications, privacy, and the need for human oversight to ensure the accuracy and appropriateness of content. === Slide 7 ===
=== Slide 7 === Literature review\nApplications of LLMs in education include generating learning materials, providing instant tutoring support, and reducing instructor workload.\nRetrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has emerged as a prominent solution to address key challenges faced by LLMs, such as hallucination and outdated knowledge, by integrating external databases to increase accuracy and credibility in knowledge-intensive tasks\nIn the educational context, RAG has significant potential to improve various aspects of learning and teaching, such as creating personalized learning content, powering intelligent tutoring systems, and supporting curriculum development, assessment, and research[24-26].\nA. Abd-Alrazaq, R. AlSaad, D. Alhuwail, A. Ahmed, P. M. Healy, S. Latifi, S. Aziz, R. Damseh, S. Alabed Alraz
opment, assessment, and research[24-26].\nA. Abd-Alrazaq, R. AlSaad, D. Alhuwail, A. Ahmed, P. M. Healy, S. Latifi, S. Aziz, R. Damseh, S. Alabed Alraz ak, and J. Sheikh, “Large Language Models in Medical Education: Opportunities, Challenges, and Future Directions,” JMIR Med Educ, vol. 9, pp. e48291, Jun 1, 2023.\nC. D. Kloos, C. Alario-Hoyos, I. Estévez-Ayres, P. Callejo-Pinardo, M. A. Hombrados-Herrera, P. J. Muñoz-Merino, P. M. Moreno-Marcos, M. Muñoz-Organero, and M. B. Ibáñez, "How can Generative AI Support Education?." pp. 1-7.\nJ. Chen, H. Lin, X. Han, and L. Sun, "Benchmarking large language models in retrieval-augmented generation." pp. 17754-17762.\nM. Kang, S. Lee, J. Baek, K. Kawaguchi, and S. J. Hwang, “Knowledge-augmented reasoning distillation for small language models in knowledge-intensive tasks,” Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, vol. 36, 2024\nA. Asai, Z. Wu, Y. Wang, A. Sil, and H. Hajishirzi, “Self-rag: L
ge-intensive tasks,” Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, vol. 36, 2024\nA. Asai, Z. Wu, Y. Wang, A. Sil, and H. Hajishirzi, “Self-rag: L earning to retrieve, generate, and critique through self-reflection,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2310.11511, 2023.\nZ. J. Wang, and D. H. Chau, "MeMemo: On-device Retrieval Augmentation for Private and Personalized Text Generation." pp. 2765-2770.\nQ. Huang, S. Fu, X. Liu, W. Wang, T. Ko, Y. Zhang, and L. Tang, “Learning retrieval augmentation for personalized dialogue generation,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.18847, 2024.
W. Wang, T. Ko, Y. Zhang, and L. Tang, “Learning retrieval augmentation for personalized dialogue generation,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.18847, 2024. === Slide 8 === Local Deployment of RAG Systems and Personalized Learning\nLocally deployed RAG: \nOn-premises RAG system enhances data security and control. Proposed architecture: \nRetrieval module for efficient access,   generation module for tailored content, decision module for retrieval-generation mediation. === Slide 9 ===
Retrieval module for efficient access,   generation module for tailored content, decision module for retrieval-generation mediation. === Slide 9 === How RAG Enhances Personalized Learning\nDynamic Content Generation\nCustomized to individual student needs\nAdapts complexity with student progress\nAddresses specific weaknesses\n2.    Real-Time Assistance\nImmediate support for student queries\nMaintains learning flow\nRetrieves and explains relevant information instantly\n3. Adaptive Learning Paths\nPersonalizes topic sequence\nAdjusts based on student performance\nEnsures efficient and effective learning\n4. Engaging Interactive Learning\nCreates context-aware interactions\nGenerates personalized scenarios\n5. Supporting Teachers and Educators === Slide 10 ===
ngaging Interactive Learning\nCreates context-aware interactions\nGenerates personalized scenarios\n5. Supporting Teachers and Educators === Slide 10 === Advantages and Architecture of Locally Deployed RAG Systems\nReasons for deploying locally hosted RAG systems in educational institutions include data security and privacy, customization and alignment, performance optimization, seamless integration, offline capabilities, and regulatory compliance\nThe system architecture for a personalized learning agent based on a local RAG system starts with defining learning goals, followed by data selection and preprocessing === Slide 11 === What is the Infrastructure need for local deployment RAG？\nInfrastructure Needs for Local Deployment of RAG === Slide 12 === Infrastructure Needs for Local Deployment of RAG === Slide 13 ===
al deployment RAG？\nInfrastructure Needs for Local Deployment of RAG === Slide 12 === Infrastructure Needs for Local Deployment of RAG === Slide 13 === Teaching Case \nConstitutional Law Research\nTask: A law student is researching a constitutional law issue involving a challenge to a state statute.\nRAG Input: The student inputs the relevant facts of the case, including the statute at issue and the constitutional provisions implicated.\nRAG Output: The RAG system searches through a locally deployed database of constitutional law cases and returns a list of relevant precedents, including cases that have addressed similar constitutional issues in the past.\nResult: The student is able to identify a key precedent that establishes a controlling principle of constitutional law, which helps to resolve the issue.\n Case Law Research\nTask: A law student is researching a case involving a contract dispute between two companies.\nRAG Input: The student in
the issue.\n Case Law Research\nTask: A law student is researching a case involving a contract dispute between two companies.\nRAG Input: The student in puts the relevant facts of the case, including the parties involved, the contract terms, and the disputed issues.\nRAG Output: The RAG system searches through a locally deployed database of case law and returns a list of relevant precedents, including summaries of the cases and links to the full text of the opinions.\nResult: The student is able to quickly and accurately identify relevant precedents that support their argument, including a landmark case that establishes a key principle of contract law.
y and accurately identify relevant precedents that support their argument, including a landmark case that establishes a key principle of contract law. === Slide 14 ===
=== Slide 14 === Future outlook and opportunities\nPractical Considerations for RAG Deployment in Education\nData Management and Preprocessing\nEffective data management and preprocessing are essential, including sourcing and curating relevant knowledge sources, cleaning and preprocessing data, and implementing efficient indexing and retrieval strategies.\nModel Serving and Inference\nRobust model serving and inference capabilities are required to handle real-time requests from students and teachers, leveraging techniques like batching, caching, and offline generation.\nMonitoring and Maintenance\nComprehensive logging and monitoring infrastructure are crucial for maintaining and troubleshooting RAG deployments, enabling performance optimization and model updates.\nAdvantages of RAG for Education\nLeveraging Exter
maintaining and troubleshooting RAG deployments, enabling performance optimization and model updates.\nAdvantages of RAG for Education\nLeveraging Exter nal Knowledge: RAG models can access and leverage external knowledge sources beyond the fixed training data, allowing them to provide more up-to-date and comprehensive information to students.\nSample Efficiency: RAG models can be more sample-efficient, as they can utilize existing knowledge bases rather than relying solely on limited training data. This can be particularly beneficial in education where data may be scarce.\nImproved Performance: By combining retrieval and generation capabilities, RAG models can outperform traditional language models in tasks like question answering, content generation, and personalized learning.
n capabilities, RAG models can outperform traditional language models in tasks like question answering, content generation, and personalized learning. === Slide 15 === Future outlook and opportunities\nFuture Directions and Opportunities\n    Multilingual and Cross-Lingual Applications：\n    Deploying RAG models for multilingual and cross-lingual educational applications, addressing challenges like linguistic diversity and cultural context.\n Multimodal Data Integration：\nIncorporating multimodal data sources, such as images, videos, and structured data, into RAG models to enable more comprehensive and engaging educational experiences.\nPrivacy-Preserving and Secure Deployment：\nDeveloping privacy-preserving and secure deployment strategies for RAG models in educational settings, ensuring the protection of sensitive student data and information. === Slide 16 === === Slide 17 === Q&A\nThank you for your listening
=== Slide 1 === Yapay Zeka Nedir? === Slide 2 === === Slide 3 === Yapay Zeka’nın Tarihçesi\nYapay zekanın babası olarak kabul edilen Alan Turing, makinelerin düşünüp düşünemeyeceğini sorgulayan ünlü Turing Testini geliştirdi 1950’ler ve 1960’lar:insan-makine etkileşiminin ilk örneklerini sunuyordu.\n1970’ler: temel araştırmalar devam etti.\n1980’ler ve 1990’lar, teknolojinin hızla ilerlediği ve bu alana olan ilginin zirve yaptığı bir dönemi temsil eder. makine öğrenimi, daha karmaşık görevleri yerine getirebilir hale geldi === Slide 4 ===
ve bu alana olan ilginin zirve yaptığı bir dönemi temsil eder. makine öğrenimi, daha karmaşık görevleri yerine getirebilir hale geldi === Slide 4 === 2000’ler\nDerin öğrenme devrimi: Görüntü ve ses tanıma, doğal dil işleme ve oyun stratejilerinde derin öğrenme, yapay zekanın çığır açan ilerlemeler kaydetmesini sağladı.\nYapay zeka uygulamalarının genişlemesi: Yapay zeka, otomasyon, sağlık hizmetleri, eğitim, finans ve daha pek çok alanda kullanılmaya başlandı. Bu genişleme, yapay zekanın günlük yaşamın vazgeçilmez bir parçası haline gelmesine yol açtı. === Slide 5 === === Slide 6 === === Slide 7 === Makine Öğrenimi === Slide 8 === Derin Öğrenme === Slide 9 === === Slide 10 === === Slide 11 === === Slide 12 === === Slide 13 === Yapay zeka teknolojileri === Slide 14 === === Slide 15 === === Slide 16 === === Slide 17 === Yapay zeka === Slide 18 === === Slide 19 === === Slide 20 === === Slide 21 === === Slide 22 === === Slide 23 ===
