=== Page 1 ===\n\nSee discussions, st ats, and author pr ofiles f or this public ation at : https://www .researchgate.ne t/public ation/380876882\nEnhancing Programming Education with Open-Sou rce Generative AI Chatbots\nConf erence Paper  · May 2024\nDOI: 10.1109/MIPRO60963.2024.10569736\nCITATIONS\n8READS\n486\n4 author s:\nAntonia Š ar č evi ć\nUniv ersity of Z agreb\n1 PUBLICA TION    8 CITATIONS    \nSEE PROFILE\nIvan T omi č i ć\nUniv ersity of Z agreb\n1 PUBLICA TION    8 CITATIONS    \nSEE PROFILE\nAndrija Merlin\nUniv ersity of Z agreb\n1 PUBLICA TION    8 CITATIONS    \nSEE PROFILE\nMark o Hor vat\nUniv ersity of Z agreb\n110 PUBLICA TIONS    1,052  CITATIONS    \nSEE PROFILE\nAll c ontent f ollo wing this p age was uplo aded b y Mark o Hor vat on 25 May 2024 .
of Z agreb\n110 PUBLICA TIONS    1,052  CITATIONS    \nSEE PROFILE\nAll c ontent f ollo wing this p age was uplo aded b y Mark o Hor vat on 25 May 2024 . The user has r equest ed enhanc ement of the do wnlo aded file . === Page 2 ===\n\nEnhancing Programming Education with Open -\nSource Generative AI Chatbots  \nAntonia Šarčević  *, Ivan Tomičić  *, Andrija Me rlin *, Marko Horvat  * \n* University of Zagreb, Faculty of Electrical Engineering and Computing, Department of Applied Computing, Zagreb, \nCroatia  \nantonia.sarcevic@fer.hr , ivan.tomicic@fer.hr , andrija.merlin@fer.hr, marko.horvat3@fer.hr   \n \n \nAbstract  — This paper describes the development of an \nOpen -Source Generative AI Chatbot, utilizing free Large \nLanguage Models (LLM) to enrich the student learning \nexperience for a university course in “Introduction to \nProgramming ” .
t, utilizing free Large \nLanguage Models (LLM) to enrich the student learning \nexperience for a university course in “Introduction to \nProgramming ” . The article aims to provide a step -by-step \nguide for selecting, fine -tuning, and evaluating available \nmodels . As a first step in choosing the appropriate LLM, \nwhich provides the most accurate responses while not \nrequiring excessive computing power, the article will cover a \ndiscussion of the  advantages and disadvantages of local vs . cloud -available models . After selecting a few promising \nmodels, the next stage includes fine -tuning LLMs to answer \ndomain -specific questions using a dataset containing essential \nrules, guidelines, and explanatory content regarding the \nsubject . The crucial aspect of selecting a model was evaluating \nanswers, and in this context, both human and automatic \nevaluation techniques will be presented .
crucial aspect of selecting a model was evaluating \nanswers, and in this context, both human and automatic \nevaluation techniques will be presented . Finally, it is possible \nto enhance the model performance and accuracy by \nincorporating Retrieval -Augmented Generation (RAG) \ntechniques and exploring the influence of various factors, such \nas different vector databases, model temperatures, maximum \ntoken lengths, prompt templates, embeddings, repetition \npenalties, and chunking siz es . Our results show that chatbots \nhave significant potential to improve academic support and \nlearning efficiency, as well as personalized education in \ngeneral . Keywords – chatbots; generative models; large language \nmodels; natural language processing; education; digital \nlearning  \nI .
ducation in \ngeneral . Keywords – chatbots; generative models; large language \nmodels; natural language processing; education; digital \nlearning  \nI . INTRODUCTION  \nThe transition to university life always  represents a \ncritical stage in a higher  education, especially for first -year \nstudents  dealing with  demanding coursework in , what is to \nthem,  new and unfamiliar  university  environments . Freshmen computer science (CS) and computer engineering \n(CE) students often need help and clarification even with the \nbasic courseware issues, such as the number of points \nrequired to achieve a specific grade, or how to install a \nplugin in the required programming working environment . Although information they require is made available to the \nstudents through the official course material and web pages, \nmany of them prefer direct communication with academic \nstaff .
s made available to the \nstudents through the official course material and web pages, \nmany of them prefer direct communication with academic \nstaff . On the other hand, teachers and other faculty members \nare outnumbered by the growing number of students, and the \ngap seems to be widening as teachers face staff shortages . This ubiquitous problem has sparked the development of \nmany novel digital solutions for support of academic \nteaching, such as the automated programming assess ment \nsystems [1][2] . Recognizing th ese issues , we developed a user -friendly \nchatbot that uses open-source  artificial intelligence (AI)  \nLarge Language Model (LLM) [3] to assist freshmen \nstudents with their academic questions  on an obligatory  first-\nsemester course “Introduction to programming” [4] taught at \nUniversity of Zagreb, Faculty of Electrical Engineering and \nComputing .
ligatory  first-\nsemester course “Introduction to programming” [4] taught at \nUniversity of Zagreb, Faculty of Electrical Engineering and \nComputing . The course teaches fundamental concepts such \nas data and control structures, functions, arrays, and file \nhandling . It also covers basic software engineering \nprinciples such as algorithm design, decomposition, \ndocumentation, and the software development li fecycle, \ngiving students the skills  to succeed in practical \nprogramming and problem -solving . The goal of this paper is to thoroughly describe the \nprocess of  theoretical exploration and hands -on training of \nLarge Language Models (LLMs) as part of our project . Our \nmotivation is to share  the results and methodologies  of the \nproject , thereby facilitating the development of similar AI -\nbased solutions for educational purposes  by other \nresearchers .
ethodologies  of the \nproject , thereby facilitating the development of similar AI -\nbased solutions for educational purposes  by other \nresearchers . The project was carried out by a joint team of \n16 undergraduate and graduate students from the University \nof Zagreb, Faculty of Electrical Engineering and Comp uting  \nunder supervision of their selected mentor  as the fourth \nauthor . The team leaders of undergraduate and graduate \nstudent teams were the first and second author, respectively . To be able to successfully complete the project we \nconsidered all open LLMs available in October 2023 . For \ninjecting domain -specific knowledge into LLM, we \nexplored both fine -tuning and knowledge augmentation \nmechanisms like Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)  \n[5][6] . Our findings favor RAG over fine -tuning for its \nsuperior performance in handling existing and new \nknowledge across different problem  domains [7][8] .
findings favor RAG over fine -tuning for its \nsuperior performance in handling existing and new \nknowledge across different problem  domains [7][8] . The remainder of the paper is organized as follows; \nSection 2 examines LLM selection criteria, focusing on \narchitecture, courseware training data, and performance . Section 3 evaluates local versus cloud LLMs against \ntechnical and financial considerations . Section 4 outlines our \nmethodology for training and fine -tuning LLMs with \nspecific datasets . Section 5 assesses model effectiveness \nusing human and automated metrics . Section 6 discusses \nRAG and its impact on chatbot performance . Finally, we \nshare our find ings and recommendations for LLM chatbot \ndeployment in education . II .
sses \nRAG and its impact on chatbot performance . Finally, we \nshare our find ings and recommendations for LLM chatbot \ndeployment in education . II . MODEL SELECTION  \nThe initial step in our project involved an in -depth \nexploration of available LLMs  – both commercial and \nMIPRO 2024/SP\n2367\n\n\n=== Page 3 ===\n\nlocated in the cloud , and free and accessible locally – \nfocusing on understanding the current landscape of high -end \nlanguage models . The factors we considered  are: \navailability, price per token, model architecture, training \ndata requirements , reported past performance, and fine -\ntuning capabilities . This comprehensive understanding was \ncrucial in identifying key characteristics that would \ninfluence the effectiveness  and usability  of LLMs .
s . This comprehensive understanding was \ncrucial in identifying key characteristics that would \ninfluence the effectiveness  and usability  of LLMs . After an \nexhaustive  valuation process, we carefully selected a group \nof model s that demonstrated not only the ability to \ncomprehend but also produce d high-quality, contextually \nrelevant responses  to courseware questions . A . Local vs cloud -available LLMs  \nOur exploration extended to considering models of \nvarious sizes, both cloud -available and locally run  [3][9] . Multiple cloud -based LLMs were tested, using cloud \nplatforms such as  the AI-powered  open platform  Poe.com  \n[10], alongside locally run LLMs from Hugging Face  online \nrepository [11] . This adapted  approach allowed us to \nthoroughly assess the performance of different models in \ninjecting domain -specific knowledge .
pository [11] . This adapted  approach allowed us to \nthoroughly assess the performance of different models in \ninjecting domain -specific knowledge . However, considering \nour infrastructure limitations, very large models could not be \nchosen because of the computing power necessary to use \nthem, and cloud -based models were unfit as their usage is \noften too expensive . In line with that, we selected a group of \n10 perspective locally run LLMs of roughly the same size (7 \nbillion parameters)  that should be further evaluated to select \none optimal model . B . Training and testing  \nFollowing the initial model selection, the hands -on \ntraining phase commenced, where our team engaged in a \nprocess of training on the official documentation of the \n“Introduction to Programming ” course, a n obligatory \nfreshman  course  at the undergraduate Study Programmes  \nComputing and Electrical Engineering and Information \nTechnology  [4] .
rse, a n obligatory \nfreshman  course  at the undergraduate Study Programmes  \nComputing and Electrical Engineering and Information \nTechnology  [4] . Specifically, pre-trained LLMs were fine-\ntuned  utilizing  available course  materials contain ing \ninstructions for installing local extensions, instructions for \ncommand prompt environment,  instructions for performing \nlaboratory exercises, and the overall student guide . In total, \nthe LLMs were fine-tuned  on a dataset cont aining  6 \ndocuments with 12.828 words . The objective was to equip each of the 10 selected  \nlanguage model s with a foundational understanding of the \nsubject matter, enabling them to generate accurate and \ncontextually relevant responses to user queries . The models \nwere then tested on a set of 20 questions used to narrow the pool of models . The answers given by the models were \nevaluated by our student team .
s \nwere then tested on a set of 20 questions used to narrow the pool of models . The answers given by the models were \nevaluated by our student team . It was immediately clear that \nsome models performed significantly worse than others . This brief evaluation was not precisely structured, but a \nrough review of models that gave us a clear picture of which \nmodels should be further tested and which should be \ndiscarded . Therefore,  we continued our evaluation with  a \nsubgroup consisting of  the following 5 models:  \n1 . llama -2-7b-chat.Q5_K_M  \n2 . mistral -7b-instruct -v0.1.Q5_K_M  \n3 . mistral -7b-openorca.Q4_0  \n4 . zephyr -7b-alpha.Q5_K_M  \n5 . zephyr -7b-beta.Q5_K_M  \nTo further scrutinize the shortlisted models, we \nexpanded the set of  evaluation  questions, introducing a \nspectrum of difficulty levels .
.Q5_K_M  \nTo further scrutinize the shortlisted models, we \nexpanded the set of  evaluation  questions, introducing a \nspectrum of difficulty levels . We deliberately included \nquestions with contexts outside the model's training data and \nthose containing grammatical errors to assess the robustness \nof the models . In the end, the full list contained N = 92 \nquestions related to the “Introduction to Programming ” \ncourse . The questions varied in difficulty, ranging from \nsimple, straightforward queries to  more complex ones \nrequiring logical reasoning  and arithmetic calculations . This \ndiverse set of questions was then fed to the trained LLMs, \nand their responses were meticulously recorded and \nevaluated by us based on predefined metrics . In this step, t he \nwhole group of 16 students in the team participated in the \nevaluation , posing all N = 92 questions to the selected 5 \nmodels .
. In this step, t he \nwhole group of 16 students in the team participated in the \nevaluation , posing all N = 92 questions to the selected 5 \nmodels . This step is described in more detail in the next \nsubsection . It is important to note that all  model names \nfollow  the GGML naming convention, with parameter b \nindicating the model's size in billions of parameters and Q, \nK, and M corresponding to different weight quantization \nlevels . Quantization is a technique that enables LLMs to run \non consumer hardware  utilizing only  CPU inference . C . Evaluation  \nThe evaluation phase involved grading the models based \non five critical categories: fluency, coherence, relevance, \ncontext understanding, and overall quality . Each model \nreceived a Likert -scale grade from 1 to 5 in each category, \nproviding a nuanced assessment of its performance .
g, and overall quality . Each model \nreceived a Likert -scale grade from 1 to 5 in each category, \nproviding a nuanced assessment of its performance . To \nestablish a benchmark for evaluation, we created a list of 3 -\n5 expected answers for each question in the extended set . These expected answers were carefully curated b y our team, \nleveraging the context used for training the LLMs . The \nresults of the evaluation  are shown in Table I . TABLE I .
carefully curated b y our team, \nleveraging the context used for training the LLMs . The \nresults of the evaluation  are shown in Table I . TABLE I . AVERAG E GRADE PER CATEGORY RESULTS FOR EACH OF THE 5 EVALUATED  MODELS\nModel  Fluency  Coherence  Relevance  Context \nunderstanding  Overall \nquality  Average \ntotal  \nllama -2-7b-chat.Q5_K_M  4.87 4.79 4.15 4.09 3.95 4.35 \nmistral -7b-instruct -v0.1.Q5_K_M  4.80 4.68 4.34 3.73 3.70 4.25 \nmistral -7b-openorca.Q4_0  4.92 4.88 4.38 4.44 4.48 4.62 \nzephyr -7b-alpha.Q5_K_M  4.81 4.71 4.43 3.84 3.67 4.29 \nzephyr -7b-beta.Q5_K_M  4.87 4.75 4.18 4.15 4.15 4.42 \n2368\nMIPRO 2024/SP\n\n\n=== Page 4 ===\n\n \n \nFigure 1 . Box-plot with Answering Time per each of the 5 evaluated model s\nIn this phase of our work, we emphasized the importance \nof depth in the evaluation of these models .
swering Time per each of the 5 evaluated model s\nIn this phase of our work, we emphasized the importance \nof depth in the evaluation of these models . Apart from \nhuman evaluation, we calculated BLEU [12], ROUGE [13] \n[14], and diversity scores to provide more objective insight \ninto the effectiveness of the model . The metrics compare the \nanswer generated by the LLM with the corresponding \ncorrect answer to the question (our list of 3 –5 expected \nanswers) . It is good practice to perform these metrics to gain \nan objective statistical basis for making decisions in a \nproject . In our case, the metrics did not result in high scores, \nbut that can be common when the model’s answer does not \nmatch the expected one word -for-word . Considering all evaluation criteria, our choice of the best \nmodel was based on the human evaluation, and by that \nMistral -7B-Openorca significantly outperformed other \nmodels . III .
a, our choice of the best \nmodel was based on the human evaluation, and by that \nMistral -7B-Openorca significantly outperformed other \nmodels . III . RAG  CONFIGURATIONS  \nThe centerpiece of our project was using Retrieval \nAugmented Generation (RAG) to enhance the performance \nof natural language processing (NLP) tasks, such as \nanswering questions  [5] . By using RAG, our chatbot gains \naccess to more useful information from a database larger \nthan the text it was trained on  [5] . Essentially, RAG is a \nknowledge augmentation mechanism for LLMs, it \nsupplements their inherent capabilities with information \nretrieved from external knowledge sources  [5] . To improve this method and find the optimal \nconfiguration we concluded detailed research on this \ntechnique and implemented and tested various combinations \nof different retriever types, chunking sizes and overlaps, \nnumber of retrievals, and different embedd ings .
emented and tested various combinations \nof different retriever types, chunking sizes and overlaps, \nnumber of retrievals, and different embedd ings . To \nunderstand how this works, a brief explanation of RAG is \nneeded . A . Retrieval -Augmented Generation  \nRetrieval -Augmented Generation (RAG) is a \nmethodology used to enhance the capabilities of Large \nLanguage Models (LLMs) by incorporating knowledge from external databases  [15] . It addresses challenges faced \nby LLMs, such as hallucination, outdated knowledge, and \nnon-transparent reasoning processes, by seamlessly \nintegrating domain -specific information . RAG improves the \naccuracy and credibility of models, particularly in \nknowled ge-intensive tasks, by synergistically merging the \nintrinsic knowledge of LLMs with the vast, dynamic \nrepositories of external databases .
knowled ge-intensive tasks, by synergistically merging the \nintrinsic knowledge of LLMs with the vast, dynamic \nrepositories of external databases . This comprehensive \napproach allows for continuous knowledge updates and \nfacilitates the integration of domain -specific  information  [6] . RAG is the process of optimizing the output of a large \nlanguage model, so it references an authoritative knowledge \nbase outside of its training data sources before generating a \nresponse [5] . RAG implements three main components: 1) \ndocument chunking, 2) embedding, and 3) searching [16] . Chunking involves dividing documents into parts of \narbitrary size [16] . The goal of this process is to achieve a \nbalance between the amount of information and the amount \nof noise contained in each chunk .
ary size [16] . The goal of this process is to achieve a \nbalance between the amount of information and the amount \nof noise contained in each chunk . Intuitively, choosing a \nchunk that is too large results in an excessive amount of \ninformation, and therefore noise, that c annot be represented \nas a vector of a specific size . On the other hand, choosing a \nchunk that is too small leads to dispersed information across \nmany chunks, which may be difficult to manage . In this \ncontext, we implemented small chunk sizes of 500 tokens \nwith an overlap of 50 tokens, medium of 1000 tokens with \nan overlap 200, and large chunks of 2000 tokens with an \noverlap of 400 . Embedding involves representing the obtained chunks as \nvectors of a specific size [16] . To convert text into vectors, \nwe use a method called contextual embedding . We wish to \nretain the word order – sentences can take on different \nmeanings depending on the word order .
use a method called contextual embedding . We wish to \nretain the word order – sentences can take on different \nmeanings depending on the word order . We also wish to \nretain the context, because the same word can have a \ndiffe rent representation depending on the words it is \nsurrounded by, meaning that sentences containing similar \nwords can have significantly different vectors . In our \nexperimentation, we employed the three following \nembeddings: 1) all -MiniLM -L6-v2 which maps sen tences \nand paragraphs to a 384 -dimensional dense vector space and \nMIPRO 2024/SP\n2369\n\n\n=== Page 5 ===\n\noffers both speed and quality, 2) Jina embedding which \nsupports longer sequence lengths, and 3) BAAI general embedding that can map any text to a low -dimensional dense \nvector . Figure 1 .
mbedding which \nsupports longer sequence lengths, and 3) BAAI general embedding that can map any text to a low -dimensional dense \nvector . Figure 1 . An overview of a typical RAG arhitecture\nIn the searching  component  vector representations of \nqueries and document chunks are used to find relevant \ndocuments . Metrics like L2 -Norm and cosine similarity \nmeasure the distance or similarity between vectors, focusing \non properties like length or direction  [17][18] . Indices are \nthen employed to search the vector database for relevant \ndocuments . Different methods, such as exhaustive search \nand Voronoi cells, balance search speed, accuracy, and \nmemory usage . Exhaustive search checks all vectors, \nguaranteeing  perfect search quality but scaling with vector \nnumber and dimension . Voronoi cells group neighboring \nvectors to expedite search, with fewer cells to explore \ncompared to vectors .
ng with vector \nnumber and dimension . Voronoi cells group neighboring \nvectors to expedite search, with fewer cells to explore \ncompared to vectors . This approach reduces search \ncomplexity but relies on efficient indexing . In our work we \nused L2 distance metric . To test this method more comprehensively, we evaluated \nvarious types of retrievers, including parent retrievers that \nretrieve a broader set of documents and rerank retrievers that \nrefine the results based on relevance . Within this framework, \nwe explored di fferent combinations of the number of \nretrievals and the top number of retrievals, such as 5 \nretrievals with top 3 documents, 7 retrievals with top 2, and \n10 retrievals with top 4 documents . B . Evaluation  \nIn the end, we had 27 configurations with rerank \nretriever and 2 with parent retriever . Each configuration was \ngiven 30 questions, a shortened list of the original  group of  \n92 .
ons with rerank \nretriever and 2 with parent retriever . Each configuration was \ngiven 30 questions, a shortened list of the original  group of  \n92 . The answers to each configuration were evaluated by our team based on new criteria focusing on expression and logic, \naccuracy and relevance, and overall quality and engagement . This approach not only ensures a thorough analysis of the \nmodel's linguistic capabilities but also delves into its \ncontextual understan ding and engagement wit h users . The expression and logic evaluation criteria provide an \nassessment of the model's ability to formulate answers \nclearly and coherently . This analysis, which is divided into \nfive levels from low to high, provides an indication of the \nmodel's ability to conve y information effectively . Accuracy and relevance as further evaluation measures \nrelate directly to the content performance of the model .
to conve y information effectively . Accuracy and relevance as further evaluation measures \nrelate directly to the content performance of the model . The \nfive-level rating, which ranges from low to high, takes into \naccount the accuracy of the information provided, its \nrelevance to the prompt, a nd the model's understanding of \nthe content . The third criterion, overall quality,  and engagement, \ncombines the ratings of expression, logic, accuracy,  and \nrelevance . This approach provides a comprehensive \nassessment of the language model's response, considering  \nboth technical accuracy and the ability to captivate and \nengage the reader . We improve the depth of our analysis of the chosen \nmodel while demonstrating our commitment to \nunderstanding the language model's performance in real -\nworld scenarios .
of our analysis of the chosen \nmodel while demonstrating our commitment to \nunderstanding the language model's performance in real -\nworld scenarios . It conforms to best practices in model \ntesting and validation and provides valuable insig hts for \nfurther refinement and optimization . 2370\nMIPRO 2024/SP\n\n\n=== Page 6 ===\n\nC . Results  \nAlthough different configurations give similar answers \nto simpler questions in the provided example, we can see that \nparameter selection can significantly affect the accuracy of \nanswers to more complex questions that require deeper \nunderstanding and broade r context . As a result, the \nconfiguration with the following parameters had the best \nhuman ratings:  \n• Chunk size: 1000  \n• Chunk overlap: 200  \n• Number of retrievals: 5  \n• Maximum number of retrievals: 3  \n• Embedding: all -MiniLM -L6-v2 \nAveraging a total mean rating of 4.13 with an average \nduration per question of 66.55 seconds1 .
r of retrievals: 3  \n• Embedding: all -MiniLM -L6-v2 \nAveraging a total mean rating of 4.13 with an average \nduration per question of 66.55 seconds1 . It is important to note that the context size significantly \naffects the response time . For example, the fastest \nconfiguration with parameters 500, 50, 7, 2, all -MiniLM -L6-\nv2 had an average response time of 29.96 seconds and an \noverall human rating of 3.5, while the slowest configuration \nwith parameters 2000, 400, 10, 4, Jina embedding had an \naverage response time of 235.48 seconds per response, with \nan overall human rating of 3.53 . Both configurations \nmentioned above used the rerank retriever . IV . CONCLUSION  AND FUTURE WORK  \nIn the described research, a s part of our project, we \nthoroughly investigated the available LLMs, taking into \naccount factors such as model architecture, training data and \nfine-tuning capabilities .
we \nthoroughly investigated the available LLMs, taking into \naccount factors such as model architecture, training data and \nfine-tuning capabilities . This comprehensive understanding \nhelped us select a model that not only understands bu t also \nprovides high -quality, contextualized answers . In addition, \nwe recognized the importance of overcoming challenges \nsuch as hallucinations and outdated knowledge, which led us \nto integrate the RAG methodology into our proje ct . By \nincorporating knowledge from the external dataset , RAG \nenhanced the accuracy and credibility of our chatbot, \nespecially for knowledge -intensive tasks .
porating knowledge from the external dataset , RAG \nenhanced the accuracy and credibility of our chatbot, \nespecially for knowledge -intensive tasks . Specifically, we \nutiliz ed available materials  for the course "Introduction to \nProgramming"  containing instructions for installing local \nextensions  in the working environment , instructions for \ncommand prompt environment, instructions for performing \ncourse laboratory exercises, and the overall student guide  for \nthe course . This external dataset consisted of  6 documents \nwith 12.828 words , in total . The process began with model selection, followed by a \nhands -on training phase in which the team fine-tuned  LLMs  \nusing the aforementioned official documentation from \n"Introduction to Programming" course . Following training, \nthe models were evaluated with 20 questions, yielding a \npreliminary assessment that revealed significant \nperformance differences between them .
ng, \nthe models were evaluated with 20 questions, yielding a \npreliminary assessment that revealed significant \nperformance differences between them . Five models were \nchosen for further testing based on the results of the initial \nevaluation . A fter this step, the evaluation was expanded to \n \n1 All time measurements mentioned in this document were \nobtained using a personal computer equipped with an \nAMD Ryzen7 5800H processor, 16 GB RAM and an include a more diverse set of 92 questions of varying \ncomplexity, all designed to test the models' robustness and \nunderstanding . These questions, some of which contained \nintentional grammatical errors or contexts unrelated to the \ntraining data, were presen ted to the LLMs . A team of 16 \nstudents thoroughly analyzed the responses, using \npredefined metrics to assess the shortlisted models' \nperformance in detail .
LLMs . A team of 16 \nstudents thoroughly analyzed the responses, using \npredefined metrics to assess the shortlisted models' \nperformance in detail . Models were evaluated in five key areas: fluency, \ncoherence, relevance, context understanding, and overall \nquality, with ratings ranging from 1 to 5 for each criterion . To benchmark, 3 –5 expected answers per question were \nprepared using the LLMs’ training context . Based on comprehensive evaluation criteria, including \nhuman assessment, the Mistral -7B-Openorca model was \nfound to be significantly superior to the others . In the future, we plan to extend our chatbot project to \nseveral subjects or even to the entire college curriculum . By \nextending the functionality of the chatbot, we hope to \nprovide students with comprehensive support and assistance \nthroughout their studies .
m . By \nextending the functionality of the chatbot, we hope to \nprovide students with comprehensive support and assistance \nthroughout their studies . This future direction is  consistent \nwith our overall goal of using technology to improve \nlearning outcomes and help students succeed in higher \neducation . The potential success of the chatbot in assisting \nstudents emphasizes its ability to be used in a variety of \nacademic discipli nes, not only in computer engineering . Using the basic framework created through theoretical \nresearch and practical exercises, we can customize and \nrefine the chatbot's capabilities to meet the specific needs \nand requirements of different subjects .
and practical exercises, we can customize and \nrefine the chatbot's capabilities to meet the specific needs \nand requirements of different subjects . Finally, in continuation of our previous research \n[19][20][21], we would like to integrate  the developed AI -\nbased chatbot into an adaptive e -learning intelligent agent \nthat can be used in any learning environment that enables \nremote sensing of students' physiological signals . The \nintelligent agent evaluates students' engagement and \nlearning curves by analyzing their affective states, \nbehavioral parameters, and psychophysiological features \nusing unobtrusive video surveillance and machine learning . The described system has the po tential to personalize \nlearning and improve pedagogical outcomes, which is \ncritical for creating future e -learning environments .
