Đƚ  \nProcedia Computer Science 00 (20 24) 000 –000  \nwww.elsevier.com/locate/procedia  \n \n1877 -0509  © 2024 The Authors . Published by ELSEVIER B.V . This is an open access article under the CC BY -NC-ND license  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by -nc-nd/4.0 ) \nPeer-review under responsibility of the scientific committee of the International Society for Research on Internet Interventions 12th Scientifi c \nMeeting  International Society for Research on Internet Interventions 12th Scientific Meeting (ISRII 12)  \nDesign, architecture and safety evaluation of an AI chatbot for \nan educational approach to health promotion in chronic medical \nconditions  \nAnthony Kellya,b*, Eoin Noctorc,d, Pepijn Van de Vena,b \naDept .
bot for \nan educational approach to health promotion in chronic medical \nconditions  \nAnthony Kellya,b*, Eoin Noctorc,d, Pepijn Van de Vena,b \naDept . Electronic and Computer Engineering, University of Limerick,  Limerick V94 T9PX, Ireland  \nbHealth Research Institute, University of Limerick,  Limerick V94 T9PX, Ireland   \ncDepartment of Endocrinology, UL Hospitals Group, Limerick V94 F858, Ireland  \nd School of Medicine , University of Limerick,  Limerick V94 T9PX, Ireland  \n  \nAbstract  \nThis paper presents the design, architecture, and safety evaluation of an AI chatbot tailored for educational purposes in man aging \nchronic medical conditions, focusing on Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (T2DM) . Leveraging conversational agents in health literacy,  \nthe chatbot integrates medically informed information, constrained responses, and response traceability to ensure appropriate ness \nand compliance with protocols .
ntegrates medically informed information, constrained responses, and response traceability to ensure appropriate ness \nand compliance with protocols . By utilizing ChatGPT with retrieval augmented generation (RAG) and careful prompt \nengineering, the system ensures reliable, traceable, and privacy -conscious interactions . Safety and efficacy testing revealed just \none inappropriate response (5%) in a simulated patient conversation and 15 (75%) fully appropriate responses . This study \nhighlights the potential of AI chatbots in enhancing patient autonomy, reliability, and privacy in accessing medical knowledg e for \nchronic conditions . © 2024 The Authors . Published by ELSEVIER B.V .
patient autonomy, reliability, and privacy in accessing medical knowledg e for \nchronic conditions . © 2024 The Authors . Published by ELSEVIER B.V . This is an open access article under the CC BY -NC-ND license  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by -nc-nd/4.0 ) \nPeer-review under responsibility of the scientific committee of the International Society for Research on Internet Interventions \n12th Scientific Meeting  \nKeywords:  conversational agent ; chatbot; ChatGPT ; T2DM ; diabetes; health literacy  \n1 . Introduction  \nType 2 di abetes mellitus (T2DM) is a chronic illness char acterized by elevated levels of glucose in the blood \n(hyperglycemia) that results from impaired insulin secretion, insulin resistance or both [1] .
r acterized by elevated levels of glucose in the blood \n(hyperglycemia) that results from impaired insulin secretion, insulin resistance or both [1] . The complications of \nuncontrolled T2DM can be serious, including microvascular damage to the eyes, kidneys and nerves; and  Available online at www.sciencedirect.com  \n^ĐŝĞŶĐĞŝƌĞĐƚ  \nProcedia Computer Science 00 (20 24) 000 –000  \nwww.elsevier.com/locate/procedia  \n \n1877 -0509  © 2024 The Authors . Published by ELSEVIER B.V .
patient autonomy, reliability, and privacy in accessing medical knowledg e for \nchronic conditions . © 2024 The Authors . Published by ELSEVIER B.V . This is an open access article under the CC BY -NC-ND license  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by -nc-nd/4.0 ) \nPeer-review under responsibility of the scientific committee of the International Society for Research on Internet Interventions \n12th Scientific Meeting  \nKeywords:  conversational agent ; chatbot; ChatGPT ; T2DM ; diabetes; health literacy  \n1 . Introduction  \nType 2 di abetes mellitus (T2DM) is a chronic illness char acterized by elevated levels of glucose in the blood \n(hyperglycemia) that results from impaired insulin secretion, insulin resistance or both [1] . The complications of \nuncontrolled T2DM can be serious, including microvascular damage to the eyes, kidneys and nerves; and \n10.1016/j.procs.2024.10.363 1877-0509© 2024 The Authors . Published by Elsevier B.V .
uding microvascular damage to the eyes, kidneys and nerves; and \n10.1016/j.procs.2024.10.363 1877-0509© 2024 The Authors . Published by Elsevier B.V . This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license ( https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0 )\nPeer-review under responsibility of the scientific committee of the International Society for Research on Internet Interventions \n12th Scientific Meeting\n\n\n=== Page 3 ===\n\n Anthony Kelly et al . / Procedia Computer Science 248 (2024) 52–59 53\n Available online at www.sciencedirect.com  \n^ĐŝĞŶĐĞŝƌĞĐƚ  \nProcedia Computer Science 00 (20 24) 000 –000  \nwww.elsevier.com/locate/procedia  \n \n1877 -0509  © 2024 The Authors . Published by ELSEVIER B.V .
r acterized by elevated levels of glucose in the blood \n(hyperglycemia) that results from impaired insulin secretion, insulin resistance or both [1] . The complications of \nuncontrolled T2DM can be serious, including microvascular damage to the eyes, kidneys and nerves; and 2 Author name / Procedia Computer Science  00 (2024) 000 –000 \nmacrovascul ar complications such as heart disease, peripher al arterial disease, and stroke  [1] . T2DM is caused by a \ncombination of genetic factors, related insulin secretion, insulin sensitivity, or both  [2] . Environmental factors such \nas obesity, and physical inactivity also play a role [3,4] . Consequently, the prevalence of T2DM has risen in tandem \nwith rising prevalence of the environmental risk factors  [5]; driven by global economics, trade liberalization and \nlifestyle  cha nges [6,7] .
ndem \nwith rising prevalence of the environmental risk factors  [5]; driven by global economics, trade liberalization and \nlifestyle  cha nges [6,7] . As a result, prevalence of T2DM globally has more than tripled from 151 million in 2000 to \n537 million in 2021 [8], with regions undergoing major economic, environmental and lifestyle changes such as Asia, \nAfrica and the Middle East seeing dramatic increases in T2DM incidence [6] . Awareness is a problem, with almost \nhalf of adults with diabetes being unaw are of it [8] . Once aware, education is effective in managing chronic health \nconditions like diabetes, throughout the course of the conditions, when combined with behavior ally oriented \nprograms . Conversational agents have been applied in clinical [9] and public health education settings [10], offering \nunconstrained natur al language interaction with the extremely broad knowledge base used for their training .
ealth education settings [10], offering \nunconstrained natur al language interaction with the extremely broad knowledge base used for their training . Whilst \nthe training of such models on vast data sets means that these conversational agents can converse meaningfully \nabout many different subjects, the breadth of the ingested information also means that responses can be \nunpredictable and, in some cases, plainly incorrect . As a result, the efficacy and safety of use of such systems, \nparticularly in medical applications, is a concern [11] . 1.1 . Health literacy in self-management of chronic disease  \nModifiable risk factors for T2DM, such as obesity, physical inactivity and diet, play a key role at the policy level \nwhere a multilevel systems approach can, for example, help people make healthy choices and reduce the promotion \nof unhealthy choices [7] . Education pl ays a key role in behavior change [12] .
r example, help people make healthy choices and reduce the promotion \nof unhealthy choices [7] . Education pl ays a key role in behavior change [12] . Hence, it also plays an important role \nin the self -management of chronic diseases such as T2DM [13,14]  to empower people to eng age in self -care by \ninforming them about their disease and applying this knowledge to help prevent complications of the condition [15] . Health literacy involves the ability to gather and critically evaluate health information in relation to a health \ncondition [16] . Low health literacy represents a significant concern [17] . Patient information is often written in the \nform of booklets provided by medical professionals to guide patients in managing their condition [18] . Unfortunately, this approach contributes to poor health literacy due to the limited interactivity and absence of \nopportunities for patients to ask questions [17] .
this approach contributes to poor health literacy due to the limited interactivity and absence of \nopportunities for patients to ask questions [17] . Furthermore, such materials do little to foster skills development and \nthe autonomy required for critical health literacy [14,19] . Health literacy in chronic disease management can be \nimproved by interactivity with medical literature [16] and improved comprehension by simplifying the material \n[20] . This paper proposes that such interactivity may be provided by AI chatbot based conversational agents \ninformed by medical literature . 1.2 . The role of chatbot conversational agents  \nMore generally, the use of chatbot technology to engage patients interactively by employing artificial intelligence \n(AI) and natur al language processing (NLP), is receiving increased attention [21,22] .
e patients interactively by employing artificial intelligence \n(AI) and natur al language processing (NLP), is receiving increased attention [21,22] . The adva nta ges of cha tbot \ntechnology include human -like interactivity with the knowledge base, and autonomy of access to it, potentially \nfulfilling the need for interactive questioning of health management in T2DM . Conversational agents have been \napplied in a variety of clinical and educational settings in healthcare [9] as well as providing education and support \nin chronic conditions [11] with public acceptance [23] . In particular, generative AI such as ChatGPT have an \nacknowledged role to play in public health education by providing information and answering questions relating to \nhealth promotion, disease prevention, and community health [10] . ChatGPT has been applied in T2DM patient \neducation [21,24] .
questions relating to \nhealth promotion, disease prevention, and community health [10] . ChatGPT has been applied in T2DM patient \neducation [21,24] . In an evaluation of answers to a series of questions pertaining to T2DM self management, the \nauthors [21] reported that responses were judged by clinicians as appropriate in 98.5% of cases and were considered \nto be more reliable than search engines . Generative Pretrained Transformers (GPTs) as conversational agents allow \nunconstrained conversational flexibility, autonomy in questioning and have been applied to improving health \nlitera cy [20] . However, there is concern about potential harm from responses that are erroneous [9] or toxic [25], \n\n\n=== Page 4 ===\n\n54 Anthony Kelly et al . / Procedia Computer Science 248 (2024) 52–59\n Aut hor nam e / Procedi a C om puter Science  00 (2024) 000 –000  3 \nand the source of information from ChatGPT is generally unknown .
8 (2024) 52–59\n Aut hor nam e / Procedi a C om puter Science  00 (2024) 000 –000  3 \nand the source of information from ChatGPT is generally unknown . Therefore, ChatGPT does not overcome the \nreliance on unaccredited medical information upon which patients typically rely when engaging in web -ba sed \ninformation seeking [26] . In a cross -sectional study of T2DM management with web search information, Weymann \net a l . [27], found web pages to be insufficient for T2DM management . Chatbot models  such as ChatGPT,  a re fine -tuned  Large Language Models  (LLMs ) that respond to prompts and \nuser conversation in a  question and answer format . By responding to prompts, the chat LLM can operate on a \nvariety of tasks without additional fine -tuning .
rsation in a  question and answer format . By responding to prompts, the chat LLM can operate on a \nvariety of tasks without additional fine -tuning . This makes prompt engineering (designing the most appropriate \nprompt for the task), an important aspect of chatbot design, in what has been called the pre -train, prompt and predict \nparadigm [28] . An example of a typical prompt suitable for this application may be: “- You are a health education \nchatbot for type 2 diabetes (T2DM) patients . - You only discuss the documents provided and information related to \nthem.” , which is designed to direct and constrain the LLM . RAG LLMs address the issue of medical credibility  by using specific documents as data on which to answer the \nquery .
direct and constrain the LLM . RAG LLMs address the issue of medical credibility  by using specific documents as data on which to answer the \nquery . RAG LLMs  allow attribution to a medical source of information because they process  the documents \nprovided to them  and may be constrained to answer based primarily or solely on the information contained within \nthem . RAG LLMs  can be developed to meet ethical design principles that require privacy by default and need to \ninvolve medical professionals [28] . They can be made to attribute the source of the infor mation they provide, \nthrough proper prompt engineering . Therefore, the proposed health literacy intervention should be designed with \nthese principles in mind, providing the proper safeguards over ChatGPT as a n LLM backend . Such capabilities are \nnot readily available to the public today .
inciples in mind, providing the proper safeguards over ChatGPT as a n LLM backend . Such capabilities are \nnot readily available to the public today . This paper describes a chatbot design for education in chronic health conditions that addresses these concerns by: \ni) using medically informed information for medical context; ii) constrained responses for appropriateness and to \ncomply with protocol; iii) response traceability; iv) rapid prototyping for iterative, clinical co -design; v) privacy . Since evaluation of conversational agents in healthcare for efficacy and safety is limited [11], this paper will \nevaluate the safety of the proposed chatbot when interacted with in the context of T2DM education . 2 . Design and architecture  \nUsers commonly interact with ChatGPT via the interface provided by Open AI . However, this places some \nlimitations on the designer’s ability to control interactions and ensure privacy .
via the interface provided by Open AI . However, this places some \nlimitations on the designer’s ability to control interactions and ensure privacy . Firstly, the ability of users to \ninfluence the prompt is useful for controlling the interaction for the benefit of producing tailored responses, but \nusers can set their own prompts and input prompt attacks have been demonstr ated that can result in toxic responses \n[25] . To control the interaction with the LLM the prompt needs to be designed carefully in a way that does not allow \nit to be changed by users . Secondly, the chatbot relies upon RAG that involves interrogating documents with \nChatGPT, with limited recourse to ChatGPT’s general training data . This is in contrast to ChatGPT’s normal \noperation that relies heavily on its general data . Thirdly, only medically informed documents should be used by the \nchatbot .
t to ChatGPT’s normal \noperation that relies heavily on its general data . Thirdly, only medically informed documents should be used by the \nchatbot . Since by default, Open AI allows users to upload documents, an interface design that does not allow \ndocument upload is required . Fourthly, user privacy cannot be ensured in the default Open AI interface since it \nrequires users to log -in to a personal account . This means a user’s conversations would be associ ated with their \naccount . In a custom interface design, anonymity can be achieved by ensuring the interface uses the same \ncredentials for all participants . These limitations can be overcome by utilizing Open AI’s application programming \ninterface (API), which allows direct interaction with the ChatGPT LLM using the Python programming language .
izing Open AI’s application programming \ninterface (API), which allows direct interaction with the ChatGPT LLM using the Python programming language . These four requirements were rea lised  in a custom -built interface allowing the user to ask ChatGPT questions \nspecifically about T2DM . To provide flexibility and security, the Python program incorporates the LLM prompt, the \ninterface design, communicates with ChatGPT via a secure API, and is hosted on the Hugging Face Hub cloud \nplatform [29] . === Page 5 ===\n\n Anthony Kelly et al . / Procedia Computer Science 248 (2024) 52–59 55 Aut hor nam e / Procedi a C om puter Science  00 (2024) 000 –000  3 \nand the source of information from ChatGPT is generally unknown . Therefore, ChatGPT does not overcome the \nreliance on unaccredited medical information upon which patients typically rely when engaging in web -ba sed \ninformation seeking [26] .
overcome the \nreliance on unaccredited medical information upon which patients typically rely when engaging in web -ba sed \ninformation seeking [26] . In a cross -sectional study of T2DM management with web search information, Weymann \net a l . [27], found web pages to be insufficient for T2DM management . Chatbot models  such as ChatGPT,  a re fine -tuned  Large Language Models  (LLMs ) that respond to prompts and \nuser conversation in a  question and answer format . By responding to prompts, the chat LLM can operate on a \nvariety of tasks without additional fine -tuning . This makes prompt engineering (designing the most appropriate \nprompt for the task), an important aspect of chatbot design, in what has been called the pre -train, prompt and predict \nparadigm [28] . An example of a typical prompt suitable for this application may be: “- You are a health education \nchatbot for type 2 diabetes (T2DM) patients .
8] . An example of a typical prompt suitable for this application may be: “- You are a health education \nchatbot for type 2 diabetes (T2DM) patients . - You only discuss the documents provided and information related to \nthem.” , which is designed to direct and constrain the LLM . RAG LLMs address the issue of medical credibility  by using specific documents as data on which to answer the \nquery . RAG LLMs  allow attribution to a medical source of information because they process  the documents \nprovided to them  and may be constrained to answer based primarily or solely on the information contained within \nthem . RAG LLMs  can be developed to meet ethical design principles that require privacy by default and need to \ninvolve medical professionals [28] . They can be made to attribute the source of the infor mation they provide, \nthrough proper prompt engineering .
involve medical professionals [28] . They can be made to attribute the source of the infor mation they provide, \nthrough proper prompt engineering . Therefore, the proposed health literacy intervention should be designed with \nthese principles in mind, providing the proper safeguards over ChatGPT as a n LLM backend . Such capabilities are \nnot readily available to the public today . This paper describes a chatbot design for education in chronic health conditions that addresses these concerns by: \ni) using medically informed information for medical context; ii) constrained responses for appropriateness and to \ncomply with protocol; iii) response traceability; iv) rapid prototyping for iterative, clinical co -design; v) privacy .
s for appropriateness and to \ncomply with protocol; iii) response traceability; iv) rapid prototyping for iterative, clinical co -design; v) privacy . Since evaluation of conversational agents in healthcare for efficacy and safety is limited [11], this paper will \nevaluate the safety of the proposed chatbot when interacted with in the context of T2DM education . 2 . Design and architecture  \nUsers commonly interact with ChatGPT via the interface provided by Open AI . However, this places some \nlimitations on the designer’s ability to control interactions and ensure privacy . Firstly, the ability of users to \ninfluence the prompt is useful for controlling the interaction for the benefit of producing tailored responses, but \nusers can set their own prompts and input prompt attacks have been demonstr ated that can result in toxic responses \n[25] .
g tailored responses, but \nusers can set their own prompts and input prompt attacks have been demonstr ated that can result in toxic responses \n[25] . To control the interaction with the LLM the prompt needs to be designed carefully in a way that does not allow \nit to be changed by users . Secondly, the chatbot relies upon RAG that involves interrogating documents with \nChatGPT, with limited recourse to ChatGPT’s general training data . This is in contrast to ChatGPT’s normal \noperation that relies heavily on its general data . Thirdly, only medically informed documents should be used by the \nchatbot . Since by default, Open AI allows users to upload documents, an interface design that does not allow \ndocument upload is required . Fourthly, user privacy cannot be ensured in the default Open AI interface since it \nrequires users to log -in to a personal account . This means a user’s conversations would be associ ated with their \naccount .
AI interface since it \nrequires users to log -in to a personal account . This means a user’s conversations would be associ ated with their \naccount . In a custom interface design, anonymity can be achieved by ensuring the interface uses the same \ncredentials for all participants . These limitations can be overcome by utilizing Open AI’s application programming \ninterface (API), which allows direct interaction with the ChatGPT LLM using the Python programming language . These four requirements were rea lised  in a custom -built interface allowing the user to ask ChatGPT questions \nspecifically about T2DM . To provide flexibility and security, the Python program incorporates the LLM prompt, the \ninterface design, communicates with ChatGPT via a secure API, and is hosted on the Hugging Face Hub cloud \nplatform [29] . 4 Author name / Procedia Computer Science  00 (2024) 000 –000 \n2.1 .
PT via a secure API, and is hosted on the Hugging Face Hub cloud \nplatform [29] . 4 Author name / Procedia Computer Science  00 (2024) 000 –000 \n2.1 . Cloud platform  \nHugging Face provides the server and software infrastructure to host the interface . This means that participants \ncan access the chatbot over the internet from any PC or phone browser at a specified URL, or it may be embedded in \nanother application as an iFrame . The Hugging Face softw are infrastructure includes a Python development \nenvironment and Gradio user interface libraries . For transparency, the Python code is publicly viewable on Hugging \nFace  [30], but security features such as API private key management and secure areas maintain the privacy of API \nkeys and any course documents used . The chat function takes place in Open -AI’s gpt-3.5-turbo -0125  model via the API secured with project specific \naccount credentials .
cuments used . The chat function takes place in Open -AI’s gpt-3.5-turbo -0125  model via the API secured with project specific \naccount credentials . The Hugging Face interface concatenates the prompt, user messages and AI responses to pass to \nthe ChatGPT model over the secure API, and collects the response to display in the interface . The API is secured  \nInterface  \nHuggingface  \nChat GPT \n(Open AI)  \nLogging    \n(e.g . Airta ble)  \nChatGPT \nInterface \n(Prompt, \nHistory)  \nReporting    \n(e.g . Padlet)  \nMedica l \nDocuments  \nAPI \nAPI \nhttp \nFig . 1 . Architecture of the system and sample output  \n \n\n\n=== Page 6 ===\n\n56 Anthony Kelly et al . / Procedia Computer Science 248 (2024) 52–59\n Aut hor nam e / Procedi a C om puter Science  00 (2024) 000 –000  5 \nwith a private key managed in Hugging Face’s secure environment .
8 (2024) 52–59\n Aut hor nam e / Procedi a C om puter Science  00 (2024) 000 –000  5 \nwith a private key managed in Hugging Face’s secure environment . Since Hugging Face does not store any user \ninteractions, other secured APIs allow logging of usage on a database or spreadsheet (e.g . Airtable or Google \nSheets) . The report function can be achieved by redirecting users to a collabor ative notice board (e.g . Padlet, \ncommonly used by the academic community) . The architecture is illustrated in Fig . 1 . 3 . Evaluation and results  \nTwo documents were selected for the RAG chatbot to access . The first was a patient information booklet [18], \nthe second was a document for healthcare professionals who care for T2DM patients [31] . The patient booklet was \nselected to represent a well structured patient centric document that should provide the kinds of answers T2DM \npatients are seeking .
nt booklet was \nselected to represent a well structured patient centric document that should provide the kinds of answers T2DM \npatients are seeking . The healthcare document was selected for its detailed information, providing relevant  content \nin a way that may be less structured for patients, representing more of a challenge for the chatbot to source from and \noutput patient centric text . For evaluation, the chatbot was asked questions related to diabetes self -management . Employing a similar \nmethodology to Sng et al . [24] a specialist endocrinologist asked the chatbot a free -flowing series of questions,  \nemulating a typical clinical interaction with a person who has a new diagnosis of type 2 diabetes ( Table 1) .
a free -flowing series of questions,  \nemulating a typical clinical interaction with a person who has a new diagnosis of type 2 diabetes ( Table 1) . The \nanswers were recorded and reviewed by the specialist for accuracy, classifying the answers as: a) appropriate (the \nanswer is fully appropriate); b) inappropriate (the answer is not suitable) or; c) partly appropriate (the answer has \naspects that may be inappropriate) . The source of the questions was evaluated by a researcher as being a) correctly \nmatched to the source (matched); b) incorrectly matched (unmatched); c) partly matched (meaning the information \nappears in the source but in a different location) . The source column lists the status of the evaluation and which \ndocument it was sourced from . For example: Matched (Patient) means the source was correctly matched to the \npatient booklet document .
ion and which \ndocument it was sourced from . For example: Matched (Patient) means the source was correctly matched to the \npatient booklet document . The word “General” in this column means the chatbot stated "this information was \nsourced from my general knowledge.", and “Unsourced” means no source was mentioned . As an example of a \ndetailed response, the conversation started with the input:  \n“I have just been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes and I am very worried” . The chatbot responded with: “I \nunderstand that being diagnosed with type 2 diabetes can be overwhelming . It's normal to feel a range of emotions \nsuch as loss, anger, disbelief, and fear . You may feel like life will never be the same again, question why this is \nhappening to you, or worry about the future and potential complications like going blind .
ke life will never be the same again, question why this is \nhappening to you, or worry about the future and potential complications like going blind . It's important to \nremember that with the right support and management, you can lead a healthy and fulfilling life with type 2 \ndiabetes . Some key tips to help manage your diabetes include eating a healthy balanced diet, maintaining a healthy \nweight, incorporating regular physical activity, not smoking, taking your prescribed medication, and monitoring \nyour blood glucose levels as advised by your doctor . Remember, you are not alone in this journey . Seeking support \nfrom healthcare professionals, diabetes educators, and support groups can make a big difference . It's essential to \ntake care of yourself both physically and emotionally . If you have any specific concerns or questions about your \ndiagnosis or managing your diabetes, feel free to ask .
f both physically and emotionally . If you have any specific concerns or questions about your \ndiagnosis or managing your diabetes, feel free to ask . It's important to take things one step at a time and prioritize \nyour health and well -being . This information was sourced from "DIABETES IRELAND – Living Well with Type 2 Diabetes" in the section \n"Recently Diagnosed with Diabetes" and "Top Tips to help Manage Your Diabetes."” . Table 1 . Free-flow Questions with Evaluation  \nYƵĞƐƚŝŽŶ  ǀĂůƵĂƚŝŽŶ \nŽŶƚĞŶƚ ^ŽƵƌĐĞ\n/ŚĂǀĞũƵƐƚďĞĞŶĚŝĂŐŶŽƐĞĚǁŝƚŚƚǇƉĞϮĚŝĂďĞƚĞƐĂŶĚ/ĂŵǀĞƌǇǁŽƌƌŝĞĚ ƉƉƌŽƉƌŝĂƚĞ  DĂƚĐŚĞĚ;WĂƚŝĞŶƚͿ \ntŝůů/ŐŽďůŝŶĚĨƌŽŵĚŝĂďĞƚĞƐ  ƉƉƌŽƉƌŝĂƚĞ  DĂƚĐŚĞĚ;WĂƚŝĞŶƚͿ \ntŚĂƚĂƌĞƚŚĞĐŚĂŶĐĞƐĚĞǀĞůŽƉŝŶŐĚŝĂďĞƚĞƐĞǇĞĚŝƐĞĂƐĞ  ƉƉƌŽƉƌŝĂƚĞ  DĂƚĐŚĞĚ;WĂƚŝĞŶƚͿ \n\n\n=== Page 7 ===\n\n Anthony Kelly et al .
DĂƚĐŚĞĚ;WĂƚŝĞŶƚͿ \ntŚĂƚĂƌĞƚŚĞĐŚĂŶĐĞƐĚĞǀĞůŽƉŝŶŐĚŝĂďĞƚĞƐĞǇĞĚŝƐĞĂƐĞ  ƉƉƌŽƉƌŝĂƚĞ  DĂƚĐŚĞĚ;WĂƚŝĞŶƚͿ \n\n\n=== Page 7 ===\n\n Anthony Kelly et al . / Procedia Computer Science 248 (2024) 52–59 57 Aut hor nam e / Procedi a C om puter Science  00 (2024) 000 –000  5 \nwith a private key managed in Hugging Face’s secure environment . Since Hugging Face does not store any user \ninteractions, other secured APIs allow logging of usage on a database or spreadsheet (e.g . Airtable or Google \nSheets) . The report function can be achieved by redirecting users to a collabor ative notice board (e.g . Padlet, \ncommonly used by the academic community) . The architecture is illustrated in Fig . 1 . 3 . Evaluation and results  \nTwo documents were selected for the RAG chatbot to access . The first was a patient information booklet [18], \nthe second was a document for healthcare professionals who care for T2DM patients [31] .
o access . The first was a patient information booklet [18], \nthe second was a document for healthcare professionals who care for T2DM patients [31] . The patient booklet was \nselected to represent a well structured patient centric document that should provide the kinds of answers T2DM \npatients are seeking . The healthcare document was selected for its detailed information, providing relevant  content \nin a way that may be less structured for patients, representing more of a challenge for the chatbot to source from and \noutput patient centric text . For evaluation, the chatbot was asked questions related to diabetes self -management . Employing a similar \nmethodology to Sng et al . [24] a specialist endocrinologist asked the chatbot a free -flowing series of questions,  \nemulating a typical clinical interaction with a person who has a new diagnosis of type 2 diabetes ( Table 1) .
