===\n\n Anthony Kelly et al . / Procedia Computer Science 248 (2024) 52–59 59 Aut hor nam e / Procedi a C om puter Science  00 (2024) 000 –000  7 \n \n5 . Conclusion  \nA custom designed chatbot has been demonstrated, building on the capabilities of ChatGPT to help improve \nhealth literacy in the management of T2DM . The chatbot fulfilled the requirements to use medically informed \ninformation, constrain responses and respect privacy, in a flexible development environment . When evaluated on a specialist led simulated patient conversation, 75% of the answers (15) were evaluated to be \nfully appropriate and 20% (5) as partially appropriate . Only one answer (5%) was evaluated as inappropriate . This evaluation shows the fea sibility  of the intervention . The few inappropriate and partly appropriate responses \nwere due to the RAG chatbot straying away from the contents of the documents sourced by it .
. The few inappropriate and partly appropriate responses \nwere due to the RAG chatbot straying away from the contents of the documents sourced by it . Forcing the chatbot to \nanswer exclusively from the source documents could be quite limiting in the breadth of health literacy patients need . Future work may  trial this approach with patients and  examine other methods to ensure the chatbot balances the \nneeds of medical rigor and patient autonomy in health literacy . References  \n[1] Welch A, Vella A . What is type 2 diabetes ? Medicine (United Kingdom) 2022;50:625 –31 . https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpmed.2022.07.003 . [2] Malone JI, Hansen BC . Does obesity cause type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) ? Or is it the opposite ? Pediatr Diabetes 2019;20:5 –9 . https://doi.org/10.1111/PEDI.12787 . [3] Reed J, Bain S, Kanamarlapudi V .
