erms of use apply . Rights reserved . === Page 11 ===\n\n7851\n Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:7841–7862 \nquestion was more difficult . Table  4 shows descriptive statistics of the number of \nwords in the tutor’s response . Table  5 presents the assessment of two instructors who taught different sections \nof the same quantitative research methods course, using the same course notes that \nserved as the source documents for the RAG-based statistics tutor developed in this \nstudy . For most questions, both evaluators agreed that the RAG-based tutor delivered \nresponses that were both sufficient and accurate (score = 7) . Although the tutor’s \nresponses to two questions (Q2 and Q5) were found to be insufficient, they did not \ncontain any incorrect information . This result suggests that the RAG approach effec-\ntively addresses the issue of hallucination commonly associated with LLMs .
incorrect information . This result suggests that the RAG approach effec-\ntively addresses the issue of hallucination commonly associated with LLMs . The \nresponses to questions Q8, Q10 and Q12 were noted by one or both instructors to \ncontain excessive information, which could potentially confuse students . The Quadratic Weight Kappa (QWK), a measure of agreement between two \nraters classifying items into mutually exclusive categories (Cohen, 1968), was calcu-\nlated to assess inter-rater reliability . QWK extends Cohen’s Kappa by more heavily \npenalizing disagreements proportionally to the squared distance between categories . Table 3  Stat tutor’s response to evaluation questions\nQuestion Tutor Response\nQ1 ```{r}\nmean(df$column_name)\n```\nReplace `column_name` with the name of the specific column in the data frame `df` for which you \nwant to compute the mean .
}\nmean(df$column_name)\n```\nReplace `column_name` with the name of the specific column in the data frame `df` for which you \nwant to compute the mean . Q2 We can use an independent samples t-test when comparing mean values from two groups . Q3 We can use t-tests to compare mean values from two groups . There are two different types of t-tests: \nindependent samples t-test and paired-samples t-test . The independent samples t-test is used when \nthe two groups are not related, while the paired-samples t-test is used when the two groups are \nrelated . Q4 Descriptive statistics commonly used when performing a t-test include measures such as the mean \nand standard deviation . These statistics provide information about the central tendency and vari-\nability of the data, which are important for understanding the distribution of the variables being \ncompared in the t-test .
entral tendency and vari-\nability of the data, which are important for understanding the distribution of the variables being \ncompared in the t-test . Q5 The R code for visualizing data for a t -test is to use the `ggplot` function from the `ggplot2` package . You can create a histogram of the data using the `geom_histogram` function, and customize the plot \nusing additional functions like `labs` to label the axes . Q6 The R code for performing an independent samples t-test is as follows:\n```{r}\nt.test(df_female$score, df_male$score)\n```\nThis code uses the `t.test()` function in R to perform the t-test . The function takes two numeric \nvectors as input, which in this case are the math test scores of female students (`df_female$score`) \nand male students (`df_male$score`) .
o numeric \nvectors as input, which in this case are the math test scores of female students (`df_female$score`) \nand male students (`df_male$score`) . The t-test compares the means of these two groups and \nprovides several outputs, including the test statistic value, the degrees of freedom, the p-value, \nand the alternative hypothesis . The p-value is used to determine the statistical significance of the \ndifference in means between the two groups . Content courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply . Rights reserved . === Page 12 ===\n\n7852 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:7841–7862\nTable 3  (continued)\nQuestion Tutor Response\nQ7 A paired sample t-test is used when the observations in the two groups are related or paired in some \nway . This means that each observation in one group is matched or paired with a corresponding \nobservation in the other group .
ted or paired in some \nway . This means that each observation in one group is matched or paired with a corresponding \nobservation in the other group . The purpose of the test is to determine if there is a significant dif-\nference between the means of the paired observations . On the other hand, an independent sample t-test is used when the observations in the two groups \nare independent and not related to each other . The purpose of this test is to determine if there is a \nsignificant difference between the means of the two independent groups . In terms of R code, the main difference lies in the argument passed to the t-test function . For a \npaired sample t-test, the argument "paired = TRUE" is used to indicate that the observations are \npaired . For an independent sample t-test, this argument is not needed as the default assumption is \nthat the observations are independent .
ions are \npaired . For an independent sample t-test, this argument is not needed as the default assumption is \nthat the observations are independent . Here is an example of R code for a paired sample t-test:\n```{r}\nt.test(group1, group2, paired = TRUE)\n```\nAnd here is an example of R code for an independent sample t-test:\n```{r}\nt.test(group1, group2)\n```\nQ8 To assess the magnitude of difference in means from two groups in R, we can compute an effect \nsize . One commonly used effect size measure is Cohen’s d . Here is an R code to compute Cohen’s d:\n```{r}\n# Assuming we have two groups, group1 and group2\nmean1 <- mean(group1)\nmean2 <- mean(group2)\nsd1 <- sd(group1)\nsd2 <- sd(group2)\n# Compute Cohen’s d\ncohens_d <- (mean1 - mean2) / sqrt((sd1^2 + sd2^2) / 2)\ncohens_d\n```\nIn this code, we first calculate the means (`mean1` and `mean2`) and standard deviations (`sd1` and \n`sd2`) of the two groups .
2^2) / 2)\ncohens_d\n```\nIn this code, we first calculate the means (`mean1` and `mean2`) and standard deviations (`sd1` and \n`sd2`) of the two groups . Then, we compute Cohen’s d by taking the difference between the means \nand dividing it by the pooled standard deviation . Cohen’s d provides a standardized measure of the difference between the means, where a larger \nvalue indicates a larger effect size . Content courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply . Rights reserved . === Page 13 ===\n\n7853\n Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:7841–7862 \nTable 3  (continued)\nQuestion Tutor Response\nQ9 We can determine if our data is appropriate for a t-test by checking if the data is normally distrib-\nuted . One way to assess the normality of the data is by creating a histogram and visually inspect-\ning the shape of the distribution .
mally distrib-\nuted . One way to assess the normality of the data is by creating a histogram and visually inspect-\ning the shape of the distribution . Another way is to perform a Shapiro-Wilk normality test, which \nprovides a statistical measure of the normality of the data . To assess the appropriateness of the data using R, we can use the `shapiro.test()` function . Here is \nan example code:\n```{r}\nshapiro.test(data)\n```\nReplace "data" with the name of your dataset . The `shapiro.test()` function will return the p-value of \nthe Shapiro-Wilk test . If the p-value is greater than the predetermined significance level (usually \n0.05), we can conclude that the data is normally distributed and appropriate for a t-test . If the \np-value is less than the significance level, we would conclude that the data is not normally distrib-\nuted and may not be appropriate for a t-test .
p-value is less than the significance level, we would conclude that the data is not normally distrib-\nuted and may not be appropriate for a t-test . Q10 The outcome of a t-test in R can be interpreted by examining the p-value . In R, we can use the \n`t.test()` function to perform a t-test and obtain the p-value . The p-value represents the probability \nof observing the data, or more extreme data, if the null hypothesis is true . If the p-value is less than a pre-determined significance level (usually 0.05), we reject the null \nhypothesis and conclude that there is evidence of a significant difference between the groups \nbeing compared . On the other hand, if the p-value is greater than the significance level, we fail to \nreject the null hypothesis and conclude that there is not enough evidence to suggest a significant \ndifference between the groups .
level, we fail to \nreject the null hypothesis and conclude that there is not enough evidence to suggest a significant \ndifference between the groups . Here is an example of how to interpret the outcome of a t-test in R:\n```{r}\n# Example t-test\ngroup1 <- c(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)\ngroup2 <- c(6, 7, 8, 9, 10)\nresult <- t.test(group1, group2)\n# Interpretation\nif(result$p.value < 0.05) {\nprint("There is a significant difference between the groups.")\n} else {\nprint("There is not enough evidence to suggest a significant difference between the groups.")\n}\n```\nIn this example, if the p-value is less than 0.05, we would interpret the result as "There is a sig-\nnificant difference between the groups." If the p-value is greater than or equal to 0.05, we would \ninterpret the result as "There is not enough evidence to suggest a significant difference between \nthe groups."\nQ11 ANOVA is different from a t-test in that it allows for the comparison of means across
ce to suggest a significant difference between \nthe groups."\nQ11 ANOVA is different from a t-test in that it allows for the comparison of means across  multiple \ngroups, whereas a t-test only compares means between two groups .
multiple \ngroups, whereas a t-test only compares means between two groups . ANOVA is used when there \nare more than two groups to compare, while a t-test is used when there are only two groups . ANOVA computes an F statistic and tests whether there is a significant difference in means among \nthe groups, while a t-test computes a t statistic and tests whether there is a significant difference in \nmeans between the two groups . Content courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply . Rights reserved . === Page 14 ===\n\n7854 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:7841–7862\nTable 3  (continued)\nQuestion Tutor Response\nQ12 To create a box and whisker plot using tidyverse in R, you can use the `geom_boxplot()` function . This function is part of the ggplot2 package, which is included in the tidyverse .
t using tidyverse in R, you can use the `geom_boxplot()` function . This function is part of the ggplot2 package, which is included in the tidyverse . The `geom_boxplot()` function takes several arguments to customize the appearance of the plot . Here are the main arguments:\n  - `data`: This argument specifies the data frame that contains the variables you want to plot . In the \ngiven context, the `df2` data frame is used, which contains the `gear` and `mpg` variables . - `mapping`: This argument specifies how the variables in the data frame should be mapped to \nthe aesthetics of the plot . In the given context, `x = gear` and `y = mpg` are specified to map the \n`gear` variable to the x-axis and the `mpg` variable to the y-axis . - `fill`: This argument specifies the fill color of the boxes in the plot . By default, the boxes are filled \nwith a solid color .
ble to the y-axis . - `fill`: This argument specifies the fill color of the boxes in the plot . By default, the boxes are filled \nwith a solid color . You can specify a specific color or use a variable from the data frame to map \nthe fill color . - `color`: This argument specifies the color of the outlines of the boxes and whiskers . By default, \nthe outlines are black . You can specify a specific color or use a variable from the data frame to \nmap the color . - `size`: This argument specifies the size of the outlines of the boxes and whiskers . By default, the \nsize is 0.5 . You can specify a different size value to make the outlines thicker or thinner . - `alpha`: This argument specifies the transparency of the boxes and whiskers . By default, the \ntransparency is 1 (fully opaque) . You can specify a value between 0 and 1 to make the boxes and \nwhiskers more transparent .
skers . By default, the \ntransparency is 1 (fully opaque) . You can specify a value between 0 and 1 to make the boxes and \nwhiskers more transparent . These are just a few of the arguments that can be used with `geom_boxplot()` . There are many more \noptions available to customize the appearance of the plot, such as adding labels, changing axis \ntitles, adjusting the position of the boxes, and more . You can refer to the ggplot2 documentation \nfor more details on the available options . Q13 To compute descriptive statistics (mean, median, and standard deviation) on groups for ANOVA \nusing R, you can use the `group_by()` and `summarize()` functions from the `dplyr` package . First, use the `group_by()` function to specify the grouping variable . Then, use the `summarize()` \nfunction to calculate the desired statistics for each group .
he `group_by()` function to specify the grouping variable . Then, use the `summarize()` \nfunction to calculate the desired statistics for each group . For example, to compute the mean, \nmedian, and standard deviation of a variable `x` for each group defined by a factor variable \n`group`, you can use the following code:\n```{r}\ndf %>%\ngroup_by(group) %>%\nsummarize(mean = mean(x),\n  median = median(x),\n  sd = sd(x))\n```\nReplace `df` with the name of your data frame, `group` with the name of your factor variable, and \n`x` with the name of the variable for which you want to compute the statistics . The resulting data \nframe will contain the mean, median, and standard deviation for each group . Q14 The alternative hypothesis in ANOVA is that the mean values in each group are not the same . Content courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply . Rights reserved .
pothesis in ANOVA is that the mean values in each group are not the same . Content courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply . Rights reserved . === Page 15 ===\n\n7855\n Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:7841–7862 \nTable 3  (continued)\nQuestion Tutor Response\nQ15 To perform an ANOVA test in R, you can use the `aov()` function . The formula in the `aov()` \nfunction specifies the independent variable and the dependent variable . You also need to provide \nthe name of the R object containing the data . The result of the ANOVA test is saved in a new R \nobject, which can be evaluated later . The important parts in the ANOVA test result in R are the F statistic, the degrees of freedom, and \nthe p-value . The F statistic measures the ratio of the between-group variability to the within-group \nvariability .
tic, the degrees of freedom, and \nthe p-value . The F statistic measures the ratio of the between-group variability to the within-group \nvariability . The degrees of freedom represent the number of independent pieces of information \nused to estimate the population parameters . The p-value indicates the probability of observing \nthe F statistic or a more extreme value if the null hypothesis is true . If the p-value is smaller than \na pre-determined significance level (usually 0.05), the null hypothesis is rejected, indicating that \nthere is a statistically significant difference in the mean values among the groups . Q16 The effect size statistic often used in ANOVA is ω2 (omega squared) . Q17 To compute an effect size for ANOVA using R, you can use the `effectsize()` function from the \n`effectsize` package . Specifically, you can provide "omega" as the type of effect size .
A using R, you can use the `effectsize()` function from the \n`effectsize` package . Specifically, you can provide "omega" as the type of effect size . This will \ncompute the effect size measure ω2, which represents the proportion of variability in the depend-\nent variable explained by the independent variable . To interpret the effect size, you can use the following guidelines:\n  - ω2 < 0.01: Very small effect size\n  - 0.01 ≤ ω2 < 0.06: Small effect size\n  - 0.06 ≤ ω2 < 0.14: Medium effect size\n  - ω2 ≥ 0.14: Large effect size\nThese guidelines provide an indication of the magnitude of the difference between groups . A larger \neffect size indicates a stronger relationship between the independent and dependent variables . Q18 To determine where the difference identified in ANOVA comes from, we can perform a post-hoc \nmultiple comparison test .
and dependent variables . Q18 To determine where the difference identified in ANOVA comes from, we can perform a post-hoc \nmultiple comparison test . One commonly used test for this purpose is the Tukey’s Honestly \nSignificant Difference (Tukey’s HSD) test . In R, we can use the `TukeyHSD()` function to perform the Tukey’s HSD test . This function takes \nthe ANOVA result object as input and provides the pairwise comparisons between the groups \nalong with the adjusted p-values . Here is an example of how to perform the Tukey’s HSD test in R:\n```{r}\ntukey_result <- TukeyHSD(anova_result)\ntukey_result\n```\nThe `tukey_result` object will provide the pairwise comparisons between the groups, along with the \ndifference in means and the adjusted p-values . This will help us identify which specific groups \nhave significantly different mean values . Note that the `anova_result` object is the result of the ANOVA test performed earlier in the code .
groups \nhave significantly different mean values . Note that the `anova_result` object is the result of the ANOVA test performed earlier in the code . Q19 When reporting the findings from an ANOVA test, we need to include the test statistic (F), the \ndegrees of freedom (df), the p-value, and the effect size measure (ω2) . Q20 Having a p-value less than 0.05 in ANOVA means that the observed difference in the mean values \namong groups is statistically significant . In other words, it is unlikely to observe such a large dif-\nference in the mean values if the null hypothesis (no difference in mean values) is true . Therefore, \nwe reject the null hypothesis and conclude that there is a significant difference in the mean values \namong the groups . Content courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply . Rights reserved .
t there is a significant difference in the mean values \namong the groups . Content courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply . Rights reserved . === Page 16 ===\n\n7856 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:7841–7862\nWhere, Ow(i,j) and Ew(i,j) are the observed agreement and the expected agree-\nment by chance, weighted by the degree of disagreement with W(i,j)=(i−j)2\n(N−1)2 . The \nQKT value indicates that the two instructors had a very strong agreement regarding \nthe quality of tutor’s responses (QWK = 0.884, p < .0001) . 5  Discussion\nThis study explored the development and evaluation of a Retrieval-Augmented Gen-\neration (RAG)-based statistics tutor aimed at assisting students in learning two sta-\ntistical analysis methods: t-test and ANOVA .
val-Augmented Gen-\neration (RAG)-based statistics tutor aimed at assisting students in learning two sta-\ntistical analysis methods: t-test and ANOVA . The results show the RAG approach’s \nefficacy in reducing the hallucination problem prevalent in Large Language Models \n(LLMs), while also highlighting the potential of RAG-based tutors to enhance stu-\ndent learning . A significant issue with LLMs is their tendency to produce fabricated or mislead-\ning content, a problem referred to as hallucination (Farquhar et al., 2024; Weise & \nMetz, 2023; Xu et al., 2024) . This study demonstrated that a RAG approach could \neffectively tackle this issue . The RAG-based tutor consistently provided accurate \nresponses to all evaluation questions, as verified by two human experts, without gen-\nerating any factually incorrect information .
provided accurate \nresponses to all evaluation questions, as verified by two human experts, without gen-\nerating any factually incorrect information . This indicates that the RAG approach, \nby leveraging information retrieved from validated sources, successfully mitigated \nthe hallucination problem and ensured the precision of its responses . Furthermore, the RAG-based tutor proved to be a versatile educational tool . It \nadeptly addressed questions requiring a range of understanding, from fundamental \nstatistical concepts to providing working R code for data analysis . Such flexibility \ncan allow the tutor to meet diverse learning needs . Students grappling with specific \nconcepts can turn to the tutor for a more comprehensive understanding, while those \nmore advanced may use it to quickly generate R code snippets, thus facilitating their \ndata analysis projects . While the findings are encouraging, there is room for improvement .
uickly generate R code snippets, thus facilitating their \ndata analysis projects . While the findings are encouraging, there is room for improvement . The vari-\nability in the length of the tutor’s responses, depending on the complexity and \nnature of questions, was observed . Instructors evaluating the tutor’s responses noted \nthat some answers might include too much information, providing excessive details \nthat would potentially overwhelm students . This issue is particularly relevant for \nnovice learners who may struggle to deal with too much information that exceeds \nthe scope of their questions or their current level of understanding .
ovice learners who may struggle to deal with too much information that exceeds \nthe scope of their questions or their current level of understanding . The cognitive QWK =1−∑Ow(i,j)∑Ew(i,j)Table 4  Descriptive statistics of \nnumber of words in the tutor’s \nresponseDifficulty Mean SD Median IQR\nEasy 45.25 34.49 36.50 35.25\nMedium 99.00 98.30 64.50 98.75\nHard 174.33 48.23 159.50 58.00\nContent courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply . Rights reserved . === Page 17 ===\n\n7857\n Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:7841–7862 \nload imposed by overly detailed responses could hinder their understanding rather \nthan support their learning (Sweller, 1988; Sweller et al., 2011; van Merriënboer \net al., 2003) . However, it is important to note that the RAG-based tutor can answer \nfollow-up questions, allowing students to request more concise explanations if \nthey find the tutor’s response too long or complicated .
r can answer \nfollow-up questions, allowing students to request more concise explanations if \nthey find the tutor’s response too long or complicated . This interactivity can help \nmitigate the issue by encouraging students to engage in further dialogues with the \ntutor, highlighting the importance of training students to effectively interact with \nthe RAG-based tutor . Teaching students the strategies for managing the depth and \nbreadth of information and how to request follow-up explanations will empower \nthem to use the tutor more efficiently . This would not only improve their learning \nexperience, but also help them develop the critical thinking skills necessary to iden-\ntify relevant information and engage more effectively in self-regulated learning .
o help them develop the critical thinking skills necessary to iden-\ntify relevant information and engage more effectively in self-regulated learning . The RAG-based approach applied in this study is inherently flexible, capable of \nbeing adapted to various subjects, thereby providing a versatile framework for enriching \nstudent learning experience across diverse academic fields . It would be highly mean-\ningful to conduct user studies evaluating student perceptions and the educational impact \nof interacting with the RAG-based tutor . Such studies could offer valuable insights into \nhow students engage with the tutor’s responses, revealing whether the tutor improves \ntheir engagement, motivation, and understanding of key concepts under study .
engage with the tutor’s responses, revealing whether the tutor improves \ntheir engagement, motivation, and understanding of key concepts under study . Also, \nassessing the educational impact, such as improvements in learning outcomes, retention Table 5  Quality of tutor’s \nresponses to evaluation \nquestionsQuestion Score (Instructor 1) Score (Instructor 2)\nQ1 7 7\nQ2 4 4\nQ3 7 7\nQ4 6 6\nQ5 4 4\nQ6 7 7\nQ7 7 7\nQ8 7 6\nQ9 7 7\nQ10 7 6\nQ11 7 7\nQ12 6 6\nQ13 7 7\nQ14 7 7\nQ15 7 7\nQ16 7 7\nQ17 7 7\nQ18 7 7\nQ19 7 7\nQ20 7 7\nContent courtesy of Springer Nature, terms of use apply . Rights reserved . === Page 18 ===\n\n7858 Education and Information Technologies (2025) 30:7841–7862\nof information, and problem-solving skills, would provide empirical evidence of the \ntutor’s effectiveness .
ormation Technologies (2025) 30:7841–7862\nof information, and problem-solving skills, would provide empirical evidence of the \ntutor’s effectiveness . This line of research could also help identify areas for improve-\nment in the development of GAI-based tutors, such as fine-tuning the level of detail in \nthe tutor’s responses or addressing any usability issues encountered by students . Considering that the RAG-based tutor requires an OpenAI API subscription, its \nuse is limited to educational institutions with sufficient resources, which could exac-\nerbate the digital divide in our society . One way to address this issue is develop-\ning a RAG-based tutor using an open-source LLM such as Ollama (Ollama, 2024) . This approach can democratize access to advanced educational tools like GAI-based \nlearning environments, particularly for institutions with limited resources .
ach can democratize access to advanced educational tools like GAI-based \nlearning environments, particularly for institutions with limited resources . In this research, the evaluation of the tutor’s responses was conducted by two \nhuman experts . While expert evaluation is considered the gold standard, it is \ntime-consuming and challenging to scale . Recent research by Zheng et  al . (2023) \nexamined the potential of employing advanced LLMs like GPT-4 as evaluators of \nLLM-generated content . Their findings indicate that these sophisticated LLM evalu-\nators can attain an accuracy rate exceeding 80%, which is comparable to the level \nof agreement typically observed among human judges . Comparing the assessments \nmade by human experts in this study with those performed by advanced LLM judges \ncould provide valuable insights, suggesting a more scalable solution for evaluating \nthe effectiveness of the RAG-based tutor .
y advanced LLM judges \ncould provide valuable insights, suggesting a more scalable solution for evaluating \nthe effectiveness of the RAG-based tutor . Finally, since hallucination tends to occur more frequently with topics lacking a \nclear scientific consensus (McIntosh et  al., 2024) and with more complex prompts \n(Rawte et  al., 2023), it is possible that hallucination may not be a serious issue for \nChatGPT without RAG when responding to questions about performing t-test and \nAVNOA using R . Thus, it would be meaningful to compare the responses from the \nRAG-based tutor to those from a standard ChatGPT model that does not employ RAG . 6  Conclusion\nThis study demonstrated the potential of a RAG-based tutor to support students’ \nlearning in statistical concepts, such as t-test and ANOVA, by providing relevant \nresponses without hallucination .
based tutor to support students’ \nlearning in statistical concepts, such as t-test and ANOVA, by providing relevant \nresponses without hallucination . The findings of this study highlight both the \nstrengths and areas for improvement in RAG-based learning environments . While \nthe tutor was able to provide correct answers, it sometimes produced excessive \ninformation . Addressing this challenge, along with evaluating student perceptions \nand the educational impact through user studies, will be essential for improving the \ntutor’s effectiveness . Additionally, the use of an open-source LLM offers a prom-\nising solution for making GenAI-based educational technology more accessible to \neducational institutions with limited resources, helping to bridge the digital divide . Acknowledgements The author used ChatGPT for proofreading the manuscript .
